window.__ARXIV_DATA__ = {"papers":[{"id":"2611.17336v1","title":"Back to Pixels: Lifting Latent Video Diffusion into Pixel Space","abstract":"Frontier video generation models work in a VAE latent space that compresses video in both space and time. This compression made training tractable, but now limits high-resolution, controllable, and interactive generation -- doubling resolution quadruples token count, conditions apply to groups of frames rather than individual ones, and each response must wait for the rest of its multi-frame block. We present PixVid, a simple yet effective method that lifts a pretrained latent video model to directly generate every frame in pixel space. PixVid keeps the diffusion transformer and replaces the VAE decoder with a convolutional head that renders its outputs into pixels. We find that predicting clean video rather than velocity, together with a larger noise shift, is key to quality. Finetuned from Wan2.1-14B, PixVid is competitive with the latent baseline within $\\sim 1.3$M training clips. Working framewise in pixels, it generates at $2\\times$ higher resolution with the same per-frame token count, keeps depth-conditioned video editing aligned under fast motion, and responds to each action at the next frame. Project page: https://chenshuo20.github.io/PixVid/","authors":["Yanke Song","Shuo Chen","Chris Rockwell","Guande He","Nan Liu","Desai Xie","Tianpei Gu","Yong-Hyun Park","Aryan V S","Dennis Yuren Menn","Ming-Hsuan Yang","Xun Huang"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:55Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17336v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17336v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17336v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96818,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Frontier video generation models work in a VAE latent space that compresses video in both space and time. This compression made training tractable, but now limits high-resolution, controllable, and interactive generation -- doubling resolution quadruples toke...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17334v1","title":"Unbundling Multimodal Vision Encoding","abstract":"How multimodal language models should encode visual input remains up for debate. Most models use a pretrained vision encoder, while some recent recipes train the encoder from scratch with the language model or remove it entirely. Vision encoders bundle together three design choices: a visual prior from upstream pretraining, dedicated vision capacity, and pre-fusion placement of that capacity (i.e., before the multimodal backbone). Because published models also differ in data, architecture, and compute, comparing them cannot isolate the effect of each choice. We disentangle these choices via a controlled framework where we pretrain all models from scratch on common data and compare them on visual question answering and language benchmarks at matched lifetime compute. We find that the visual prior is not necessary; an encoder trained jointly from scratch is on par with a pretrained one, without a separate training stage. What matters instead is dedicated vision capacity: an encoder-free model trails on visual understanding even when its backbone is enlarged by the encoder's parameter count. Yet that capacity need not precede multimodal fusion, as vision-only modules added in-fusion to early backbone layers perform comparably to a pre-fusion encoder and can lower time to first token. These results also hold across backbone sizes, when encoder pretraining is treated as free, and when starting from a pretrained language model. Overall, our findings favor dedicated vision capacity trained jointly with the backbone and placed early within it.","authors":["Ellis Brown","Chris Hoang","Shengyi Qian","Shang-Wen Li","Mike Lewis","Marjan Ghazvininejad","Luke Zettlemoyer","Lucas Beyer","Mengye Ren","Jakob Verbeek","Saining Xie","Hu Xu"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17334v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17334v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17334v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":206882,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：How multimodal language models should encode visual input remains up for debate. Most models use a pretrained vision encoder, while some recent recipes train the encoder from scratch with the language model or remove it entirely. Vision encoders bundle togeth...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17331v1","title":"TTB: Test-time Baking for Efficient Rendering of Decoder-only View Synthesis Models","abstract":"Decoder-only Large View Synthesis Models (LVSMs) with KV-cache have recently achieved state-of-the-art quality by regressing novel views from neural networks without reconstructing 3D geometry, while Test-Time Training (TTT) layers save computation but sacrifice quality. We propose Test-Time Baking (TTB), a strategy that learns to bake global scene information from cross-view attention into a lightweight MLP, enabling fast and high-fidelity novel-view decoding. The first key is to repurpose the fast-weight MLP of TTT to learn the cross-view attention mapping explicitly from its input-output pairs, rather than replacing attention entirely, achieving the same rendering efficiency as TTT while delivering substantially higher quality. The second key addresses a modality misalignment between context prefilling and novel-view rendering: we disentangle context tokens into camera-only context rendering tokens to serve as TTB input, and context ground-truth tokens with image content to serve as the TTB mapping target, ensuring the baked MLPs are relieved of the modality generalization challenge between context and novel-view processing. Our method achieves up to **23x** rendering speedup with quality on par with the state-of-the-art across several benchmarks. Project page: https://test-time-baking.github.io","authors":["Chin-Yang Lin","Ryo Hachiuma","Jaesung Choe","Min-Hung Chen","Yu-Chiang Frank Wang","Yen-Yu Lin","Wei-Chen Chiu","Yu-Lun Liu","Cheng Sun"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:52Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17331v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17331v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17331v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":104545,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Decoder-only Large View Synthesis Models (LVSMs) with KV-cache have recently achieved state-of-the-art quality by regressing novel views from neural networks without reconstructing 3D geometry, while Test-Time Training (TTT) layers save computation but sacrif...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17330v1","title":"ProtoVis-Nav: Prototypical Visual Imagination for Visual-Spatial Aligned UAV Navigation","abstract":"Vision language navigation (VLN) is very challenging for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) since small errors in 3D motion prediction can lead to navigation errors and collisions. Unable to fathom execution outcomes in 3D environments, existing methods often struggle to predict accurate navigation waypoints from language instructions. To solve this, we introduce the Prototypical Visual Imagination framework, shortened as ProtoVis-Nav, which forces the model to imagine an outcome-associated local visual representation for the intended next UAV movement. Specifically, we predict feature prototypes and use their linear combination to estimate the visual feature of the target region. In this manner, the VLN model first imagines the local visual representation of the next intended location, and then uses predicted prototypes as context for generating the final action, promoting alignment between visual context and waypoint outputs. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of the ProtoVis-Nav framework and show substantial improvements over existing baselines.","authors":["Guangyuan Sui","Yunzhong Hou","Chi Harold Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17330v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17330v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17330v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":68643,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision language navigation (VLN) is very challenging for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) since small errors in 3D motion prediction can lead to navigation errors and collisions. Unable to fathom execution outcomes in 3D environments, existing methods often st...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17324v1","title":"K2V: Generative Keyframing For Physically Plausible Video Generation","abstract":"Video diffusion models are able to generate samples with high-quality appearance and textural qualities. However, content produced by these methods is known to exhibit significant physical errors. This is because, in a classical video generation setting, all physical reasoning must take place simultaneously and exclusively within the diffusion process.We propose to resolve these issues by redistributing the task of physical reasoning onto a language model-backed keyframe generation module, which allows for explicit reasoning in language, and fine-grained control over the appearance of the video at important moments in the video. We then condition a video generation model with these keyframes, reducing the role of the diffusion model to the simpler task of interpolating between physically-correct intermediate states, rather than inferring an entire sequence with diffusion. Results demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms existing approaches, achieving state-of-the-art performance on the Physics-IQ Verified benchmark and PhyGenBench.","authors":["Felix O'Mahony","Daniel Olmeda Reino","Roberto Cipolla","Ayush Tewari"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17324v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17324v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17324v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":69772,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video diffusion models are able to generate samples with high-quality appearance and textural qualities. However, content produced by these methods is known to exhibit significant physical errors. This is because, in a classical video generation setting, all...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17321v1","title":"BrickFlow: Connectivity-Guided Brick Reconstruction","abstract":"Given a set of LEGO parts and an image of an assembled object, our task is to infer the pose of each part to assemble the object. In contrast with traditional 3D reconstruction, doing so requires satisfying discrete physical constraints, which provide structure but render the problem combinatorial and highly sensitive to small pose errors. To learn a prior over how parts fit together, we train a set-conditioned SE(3) flow model on procedurally generated assemblies. We then finetune this model to inject image conditioning, using 1% as much data. However, while the learned flow captures global structure, it does not enforce connectivity. To address this, we introduce an analytic connectivity-field guidance to pull predictions toward connected assemblies at test time. Through this, we demonstrate improved build quality with model size, pretraining, and guidance. We make our models and data available for research purposes at https://kulits.github.io/BrickFlow/","authors":["Peter Kulits","Cordelia Schmid"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:58:51Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:58:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17321v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17321v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17321v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":54671,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Given a set of LEGO parts and an image of an assembled object, our task is to infer the pose of each part to assemble the object. In contrast with traditional 3D reconstruction, doing so requires satisfying discrete physical constraints, which provide structu...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17318v1","title":"Spatial Foundry: A Multimodal Dataset for Spatial Intelligence","abstract":"Spatial intelligence requires connecting visual observations to the geometry, objects, and persistent state of an environment. Images alone leave these relationships implicit, while disconnected annotations make it difficult to study appearance, camera motion, and scene changes under a common reference. We introduce Spatial Foundry, a collection of 5,280 furnished residential 3D scene configurations with editable assets and aligned visual, geometric, and temporal data. Its modalities include floor plans, perspective and panoramic RGB, metric depth, point clouds, meshes, instance-level 3D bounding boxes, and camera trajectories paired with video. Shared scene coordinates link observations across views and time; editable source scenes enable additional camera paths and controlled object changes. We describe the data representation and construction workflow, and discuss applications in floor-plan-conditioned 3D generation, geometric reconstruction, instance-level perception, camera-conditioned video generation, shared world modeling, and scene editing. Controlled camera and scene variations support spatial perception and world-model research. The public project page provides videos and an interactive colored point-cloud preview.","authors":["Ethan Lin","Yang Song"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:58:36Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:58:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17318v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17318v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17318v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42661,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation, instance, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Spatial intelligence requires connecting visual observations to the geometry, objects, and persistent state of an environment. Images alone leave these relationships implicit, while disconnected annotations make it difficult to study appearance, camera motion...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17298v1","title":"Consistent Video-to-Video Translation via Explicit Correspondences","abstract":"Interactive video-to-video applications require real-time generation while maintaining long-range temporal consistency. However, recent methods achieve speed by restricting attention to a fixed-length causal window. This limits long-range consistency whenever relevant history falls outside the context window. To address this limitation, we introduce vid2vid-long, a correspondence-based approach grounded in a simple observation. Video-to-video translation provides an explicit cross-frame reference: the regions that match in the input video should match in the output video too. Concretely, we compute patch-level correspondences in the input, guiding retrieval of previously generated outputs in the corresponding regions, and condition the model on these patches through correspondence cross-attention. Our experiments demonstrate that vid2vid-long consistently improves long-range temporal consistency across recent video models while preserving generation quality and real-time performance.","authors":["Gaurav Parmar","Zhengqi Li","Richard Zhang","Jun-Yan Zhu","Srinivasa Narasimhan","Eli Shechtman","Yotam Nitzan"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:55:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:55:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17298v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17298v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17298v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77927,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, retrieval 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Interactive video-to-video applications require real-time generation while maintaining long-range temporal consistency. However, recent methods achieve speed by restricting attention to a fixed-length causal window. This limits long-range consistency whenever...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17240v1","title":"Objects as Addresses: Instance Binding in Vision Transformer Attention","abstract":"Humans naturally parse visual scenes into coherent individual objects rather than scattered features, a capability cognitive science calls object binding. While recent work shows that pretrained Vision Transformers (ViTs) intrinsically acquire this ability, how native self-attention computations mechanistically keep object instances separate remains unresolved. By studying this across eight pretrained ViTs, we found that instance separation is expressed more strongly in attention routing than in token content: selected query-key relations distinguish individual objects more reliably than value similarity or residual representations. Inside heads, patches belonging to one individual tend to read from a common image region, which we term their attention address. Visually distinct parts of one person share an address, whereas identical duplicated instances receive separate, movable addresses. These shared destinations arise in part because query interactions with the spatial structure of keys direct attention toward consistent regions for one object and elsewhere for another. In DINOv2 and most other backbones we tested, editing this query component at the selected head redirects its attention address toward another individual. Grouping patches by this query component separates same-category instances, reaching .615 FG-ARI versus .289 for the same clustering of final-layer features, which tend to merge them.","authors":["Khalil Sabri","Guillaume-Alexandre Bilodeau","Nicolas Saunier","Wassim Bouachir"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:27:11Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:27:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17240v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17240v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17240v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":86338,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Humans naturally parse visual scenes into coherent individual objects rather than scattered features, a capability cognitive science calls object binding. While recent work shows that pretrained Vision Transformers (ViTs) intrinsically acquire this ability, h...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17199v1","title":"MALNISI-Infused Graph Construction and Weighted Contrastive Loss based GAT for Unsupervised Representation Learning in HSI","abstract":"Hyperspectral images (HSI) possess discriminating spectral characteristics with respect to land utilization due to high-dimensional information. Recently, unsupervised representation learning using graph neural networks has unveiled these utilization-specific complex spectral relations. This article has three main contributions. First, it proposes an index, the Mean Absolute Local Neighborhood Inverse Spectral Index (MALNISI), which measures the similarity between two pixels based on their neighborhood information. The index is used to construct a weighted adjacency matrix, giving the HSI a graph structure. Second, it proposes an upgraded Graph Attention Network that learns compact latent representations of hyperspectral data from the constructed graph. In this network, an enhanced multi-head attention mechanism incorporates the MALNISI weights as an auxiliary feature to emphasize learned attention between neighboring pixels. To alleviate over-smoothing and preserve fine-grained spectral details, the architecture integrates residual connections, skip projections, and feature normalization, which together enhance representation capability, stabilize learning, and accelerate convergence. Third, it proposes a weighted contrastive loss function that incorporates neighborhood-aware MALNISI between connected pixels, encouraging strongly connected pixels in the high-dimensional space to remain close in the latent space, and weakly connected pixels to stay apart. The resulting discriminative low-dimensional embeddings are then clustered using spectral clustering. Experimental analysis on six benchmark HSI datasets demonstrates the efficiency and necessity of the three aforementioned contributions, and the comparative study highlights superior performance over fourteen state-of-the-art approaches.","authors":["Soumi Sarkar","Subhadip Boral","Sarbani Palit","Ashish Ghosh"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:02:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:02:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17199v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17199v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17199v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":74591,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Hyperspectral images (HSI) possess discriminating spectral characteristics with respect to land utilization due to high-dimensional information. Recently, unsupervised representation learning using graph neural networks has unveiled these utilization-specific...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17194v1","title":"eDMD: Endpoint Distribution Matching for Few-Step Diffusion Distillation","abstract":"Distribution matching distillation (DMD) has emerged as a leading paradigm for distilling diffusion models into few-step students, where an auxiliary fake score model tracks the student's distribution and the discrepancy between the teacher and fake predictions drives the student toward the teacher. In multi-step distillation, although only the student's final output is delivered, existing methods train the fake model on intermediate states or across all denoising steps. Consequently, the student's update signal becomes entangled with intermediate distributions, which can distort the matching direction and remain nonzero even after the student's final outputs already match the teacher distribution. To resolve this mismatch, we propose Endpoint Distribution Matching Distillation (eDMD), which trains a single shared fake model exclusively on the student's final outputs and uses it to correct every denoising step. Our theoretical analysis proves that this endpoint reference guarantees that the distribution matching term vanishes if and only if the student's final output distribution matches the teacher's, regardless of intermediate distributions. Beyond the fake reference, we further strengthen teacher supervision through a precise teacher (PT) that replaces single-step guided predictions with short multi-step trajectories on the final noise interval, paired with a calibrated query density for intermediate steps. Experiments on SD3-Medium, FLUX.2-klein (4B and 9B), and Qwen-Image-2.1 demonstrate that eDMD consistently outperforms existing distribution matching methods for 4-step generation.","authors":["Tong Zhao","Xianfang Zeng","Yucheng Han","Ruoyu Wang","Mingkun Lei","Gang Yu","Chi Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:57:48Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:57:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17194v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17194v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17194v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":137944,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Distribution matching distillation (DMD) has emerged as a leading paradigm for distilling diffusion models into few-step students, where an auxiliary fake score model tracks the student's distribution and the discrepancy between the teacher and fake predictio...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17190v1","title":"Consensus Capture in Reference-Based UAV Video Stabilization: A Benchmark on Dynamic Urban Scenes","abstract":"Reference-frame stabilization registers every frame of a hovering-drone video onto one reference frame, keeping object positions comparable. The registration pipelines it relies on are selected on feature-matching benchmarks in which only the camera moves. Over a busy intersection, vehicles moving together supply mutually consistent correspondences that can draw the robust homography estimate toward their motion: consensus capture, which inlier statistics cannot flag. A two-part benchmark measures it and the object masking meant to prevent it. A static protocol scores ten pipelines under exactly known homographies on 4K aerial frames, tuned on a site-disjoint split at a matched resolution and feature budget. A dynamic protocol scores them at an offline and a real-time budget on hovers selected from the video alone to span the amount and coherence of moving foreground, plus a stationary control. It measures background alignment, object position error and the share of a moving group's displacement an estimate follows, against a pseudo-ground-truth reference audited with manually annotated ground control points, which also score every candidate. On static scenes, tuned AKAZE, RootSIFT, SIFT and BRISK lead, retuning the matcher thresholds raises the mean average accuracy (mAA) by at most 0.0062, and masking costs less than 0.005. Unmasked ORB is captured on 28 of 304 real-time dynamic pairs and on 6 offline; its real-time captured estimates have a median corner error of 18.8 px yet typical inlier counts and ratios. Masking removes capture for every pipeline and leaves the leading pipelines unchanged. Code, data and results are released.","authors":["Robert Fonod","Nikolas Geroliminis"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:55:06Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:55:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":268210,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reference-frame stabilization registers every frame of a hovering-drone video onto one reference frame, keeping object positions comparable. The registration pipelines it relies on are selected on feature-matching benchmarks in which only the camera moves. Ov...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17146v1","title":"HiVE-VQA: Hierarchical Interaction via Volumetric Embedding for Video Quality Assessment","abstract":"The proliferation of user-generated video content has made accurate and robust no-reference video quality assessment (NR-VQA) a highly important challenge. Existing research increasingly relies on architecturally complex multi-branch pipelines or computationally intensive fine-tuning of single-branch backbones. In this paper, we investigate whether a single frozen video foundation model can serve as an effective NR-VQA backbone, and propose HiVE-VQA, the first framework (to our knowledge) to apply a JEPA-based video encoder to this task. HiVE-VQA extracts token grids from multiple depths of a pre-trained V-JEPA 2.1 encoder and stacks them into a hierarchical spatio-temporal volume, which a compact 3D convolutional head fuses across its layer, spatial, and temporal axes, combining low-level distortion cues from shallow layers with the semantic and temporal context captured at greater depths. The encoder remains frozen throughout, so only the head is trained: 5.2M parameters (5.7\\% of the total), with no auxiliary multi-modal models and an inference cost that is invariant to video resolution. Trained solely on LSVQ and evaluated without any fine-tuning, HiVE-VQA outperforms 17 state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods in SROCC and PLCC on both intra-dataset (LSVQ-test, LSVQ-1080p) and cross-dataset (KoNViD-1k, LIVE-VQC) benchmarks. These results indicate that hierarchical, depth-wise fusion of features from a single frozen video foundation model provides a more effective and efficient basis for NR-VQA than task-specific multi-network fusion. Code and model checkpoints are available at https://github.com/RobHam99/HiVE-VQA.","authors":["Robbie Hamilton","Fan Zhang","David Bull"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:32:10Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:32:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17146v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17146v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17146v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":53917,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：The proliferation of user-generated video content has made accurate and robust no-reference video quality assessment (NR-VQA) a highly important challenge. Existing research increasingly relies on architecturally complex multi-branch pipelines or computationa...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17111v1","title":"Spatiotemporal learning improves quantification of pore-space evolution and fluid displacement","abstract":"Pore-space evolution and fluid displacement influence how rocks transmit and store fluids, affecting geological carbon storage, underground hydrogen storage and groundwater contaminant transport. A single time-resolved micro-CT experiment may produce tens to hundreds of three-dimensional images, and segmentation is required to quantify physical properties and run image-based simulations. Traditional methods such as thresholding perform poorly on noisy, low-contrast images and need manual adjustment per image; 3D deep learning networks segment each image without temporal information, so their errors vary between images and could be mistaken for physical change. Here we introduce a transformer-based 4D segmentation framework combining spatial and temporal information. It uses sequential images to resolve ambiguous phase assignments and separate physical change from segmentation error. The method is tested on images of mineral dissolution: it measures pore-volume growth and rock boundaries more accurately than an equivalent 3D model that ignores temporal information. It also captures the increase in permeability verified against reference segmentations and flow simulations. The advantage transfers to another dissolution sample without retraining. On two other datasets of fluid displacement, it reduces spurious changes in predicted rock and porosity fluctuations and accurately identifies filling events. Spatiotemporal segmentation turns sequences of hundreds of three-dimensional images into more reliable quantitative measurements of pore-space evolution and fluid displacement without per-image parameter adjustment to support the analysis of time-resolved CT imaging.","authors":["Zhuangzhuang Ma","Rukuan Chai","Qianqian Ma","Yanghua Wang","Branko Bijeljic","Martin J. Blunt"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:04:50Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:04:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17111v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17111v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17111v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":134525,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Pore-space evolution and fluid displacement influence how rocks transmit and store fluids, affecting geological carbon storage, underground hydrogen storage and groundwater contaminant transport. A single time-resolved micro-CT experiment may produce tens to...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17105v1","title":"Predicting Ethanol Drinking Status from Nonhuman Primate Liver Histology: A Proof of Concept for Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning in Few-Shot Vision-Language Modeling","abstract":"Combining vision-language models (VLMs) with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) offers a promising approach to few-shot whole-slide image (WSI) classification in computational pathology. This study evaluates this approach for predicting ethanol drinking status from nonhuman primate liver histology. Adapters are evaluated against non-PEFT configurations on a subset of the Monkey Alcohol Tissue Research Resource (MATRR). All evaluated PEFT configurations achieve higher mean balanced accuracy than the non-PEFT baselines. For example, in the four-shot setting, attention-based multiple instance learning (ABMIL) alone achieves 43.5% mean balanced accuracy, compared with 78.1% after adding an adapter. Qualitative attention analysis shows that textual pathway adaptations produce localized attention heatmaps, while visual pathway adaptations produce more diffuse ones. A pathologist's review identified similar histological features across adaptations.","authors":["Amirmohammad Mohammadi","Rupak Khadka","Kathleen A. Grant","Mary Lauren Benton","Joshua Peeples"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:02:27Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:02:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17105v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17105v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17105v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":19062,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Combining vision-language models (VLMs) with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) offers a promising approach to few-shot whole-slide image (WSI) classification in computational pathology. This study evaluates this approach for predicting ethanol drinking s...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17104v1","title":"TuMAP: A Dual-View, Morphotype-Aware Pathomics Pipeline for Quantifying Renal Tubular Remodeling","abstract":"Quantifying disease-associated renal tubular remodeling is challenging because tubular appearances are heterogeneous. We introduce TuMAP (Tubular Morphotype-Aware Pathomics), a pipeline combining (1) dual-view foundation-model representations and provisional morphotype annotation, (2) compositional and within-morphotype pathomics, and (3) patient-level statistical inference. From 456,158 tubules in 358 PAS-stained kidney biopsy slides, TuMAP identified eight image-based morphotypes, seven of which showed stable subsampling assignments. Across 219 patients in six diagnostic groups, diabetic nephropathy (DN) and tubulointerstitial nephritis/acute tubular injury (TIN/ATI) each differed from minimal change disease in seven morphotype proportions, while IgA nephropathy differed in six. DN showed reduced nuclear solidity and increased eccentricity across all eight morphotypes. Area-normalized nuclear density exposed additional within-morphotype differences. TuMAP provides a framework for separating changes in tubular morphotype composition from phenotypic differences within morphotypes.","authors":["Ruijie Wu","Yuechen Yang","Yanfan Zhu","Yu Wang","Shilin Zhao","Ming-Tsun Tsai","Szu-Yuan Li","Shuo-Ming Ou","Tz-Heng Chen","Haichun Yang","Yuankai Huo"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:02:18Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:02:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17104v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17104v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17104v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":20856,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Quantifying disease-associated renal tubular remodeling is challenging because tubular appearances are heterogeneous. We introduce TuMAP (Tubular Morphotype-Aware Pathomics), a pipeline combining (1) dual-view foundation-model representations and provisional...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17103v1","title":"Joint Null-Space Projection for Data-Free Model Merging","abstract":"Merging independently fine-tuned checkpoints into a single multi-task model requires no data, no labels, and no gradient steps. In practice the task vectors of different tasks interfere, and the standard remedies--averaging, magnitude pruning, sign election--operate entry by entry, ignoring that each delta induces a low-dimensional update on the hidden features it acts upon. We recast merging as a subspace interference problem. For every two-dimensional weight tensor we take the top-r right-singular subspace of each task's delta--the input directions along which that task moves the representation most--and remove from each task vector the component lying in the union of the other tasks' subspaces. The resulting Joint Null-Space Projection (JNSP) is closed-form and order-independent, needs no forward pass, and is defined entirely by the checkpoint set. On eight CLIP image-classification tasks JNSP merges ViT-B/16 experts to 89.45% average accuracy without using a single image, 11.88 points above the strongest parameter-wise training-free baseline (TIES-Merging, 77.57%), 14.22 above Task Arithmetic, and 0.98 above WUDI's data-free consensus merge (88.47%). The gain holds across three backbones on the independently trained TALL release (+10.8, +12.6, +9.1 over TIES for ViT-B/16, ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14) and replicates there at 89.20%; controls confirm the source--a single shared subspace instead of per-task ones loses 2.9 points, and a sequential variant collapses to 69.6%. On RoBERTa-large the same projection underperforms plain task arithmetic, which we report rather than hide. Code is available at https://github.com.","authors":["Tian Xia"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:01:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:01:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17103v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17103v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17103v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":176818,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Merging independently fine-tuned checkpoints into a single multi-task model requires no data, no labels, and no gradient steps. In practice the task vectors of different tasks interfere, and the standard remedies--averaging, magnitude pruning, sign election--...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17060v1","title":"Ropedia Xperience 2: A Gold Standard for Scaling Physical AI Environments and Behaviors","abstract":"Large foundation models such as GPT-6 Astra and Claude Fable 5.1 have recently been shown to control robots directly from visual observations. They nevertheless remain brittle on long-horizon, contact-rich tasks in real-world environments. Further progress depends on two complementary resources: demonstrations of human behavior that supervise how tasks are performed, and interactive environments in which policies can be rolled out, evaluated, and improved in closed loop. Language models have advanced along well-characterized scaling laws; the corresponding question for physical AI is how capability scales with environments and behaviors, which first requires identifying which data to scale and how to produce and reuse such data continuously. To address this question, we introduce Ropedia Xperience 2, comprising 1,589 released metric-scale 3D scans and a collection of 4,230 egocentric recordings of 74 tasks (131 hours). We further show that each capture can be repurposed for robot learning: scenes are converted into physically interactive simulation environments, human motion is retargeted to whole-body robot trajectories, and additional robot behaviors are obtained through virtual teleoperation and automated long-horizon trajectory generation. We propose this joint capture of environments and behaviors as a gold standard for physical AI data collection, laying a data foundation for establishing scaling laws over environments and behaviors.","authors":["Hao Li","Fangzhou Hong","Zhaoxi Chen","Hui En Pang","Kairui Hu","Mingze Sun","Runmao Yao","Xinhao Ji","Xiyuan Zheng","Xuanli Chen","Yalun Dai","Yap Kim Hui","Dingwen Zhang","Yuanyuan Gao","Ziang Cao","Cheng Huang Yean","Jackson Peng","Rui Duan","Yaobo Liu","Zetong Wang","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T16:37:21Z","updated":"2026-11-12T16:37:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17060v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17060v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17060v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":78554,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Large foundation models such as GPT-6 Astra and Claude Fable 5.1 have recently been shown to control robots directly from visual observations. They nevertheless remain brittle on long-horizon, contact-rich tasks in real-world environments. Further progress de...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17048v1","title":"AURA 2.0: From User-Driven Responses to Model-Initiated Conversations in Streaming Video","abstract":"Most AI assistants respond only when prompted, remaining silent even when events in the video stream offer meaningful opportunities for conversation. To move beyond purely user-driven interaction, we present AURA 2.0, a streaming video large language model that introduces three core upgrades to AURA. The key advance is Model-Initiated Conversation (MIC), which enables the model to identify topics worth discussing in the evolving visual stream and initiate conversations at appropriate moments without a user query. Stream-Aware Tool Calling (SATC) enables the model to invoke external tools using the current visual and conversational context and incorporate returned information into the ongoing interaction. Interactive Visual Grounding (IVG) supports visual grounding in both user inputs and model outputs. The model can identify objects marked by users on video frames and produce frame-aligned bounding boxes to link its responses to specific objects and regions. To develop these capabilities, we construct a dedicated streaming interaction dataset and adopt a three-stage training scheme to integrate the new behaviors with AURA's existing streaming interaction abilities. We further incorporate Long-Horizon Interaction Memory (LHIM) to maintain contextual continuity over extended video streams. Evaluations on public streaming benchmarks show that AURA 2.0 achieves leading performance among open-source streaming models, while targeted evaluations further validate its new interaction capabilities. We open-source the model weights and inference code.","authors":["Shuhan Li","Huankang Guan","Xintong Guo","Yang Bo","Yuyang He","Dunyuan Xu","Jingyan Chen","Fang Liu","Tsz Kin Wan","Jinpeng Chen","Zhenqi He","Yuxuan Hu","Wenbo Li","Han Gao","Qijun Li","Long Chen","Hongsheng Li","Rui Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T16:31:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T16:31:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17048v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17048v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17048v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":81044,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Most AI assistants respond only when prompted, remaining silent even when events in the video stream offer meaningful opportunities for conversation. To move beyond purely user-driven interaction, we present AURA 2.0, a streaming video large language model th...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16925v1","title":"Report of the 8th LSVOS Challenge: Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding","abstract":"This report summarizes the Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding (MUMU) track of the 8th Large-scale Video Object Segmentation (LSVOS) Challenge, held in conjunction with ECCV 2026. MUMU asks for a single unified model that jointly performs multi-concept image tagging over quality, scene, and event vocabularies, open-vocabulary object detection, and concise image captioning, within a budget of 0.5B parameters and 8 GB of peak inference memory. Submissions are ranked by a weighted task score scaled by an efficiency factor. We describe the tasks, rules, and evaluation protocol, report the final results, and review the methods of the top three teams. All three leading solutions build on the compact Florence-2-base model, but extend it in different ways: lightweight tagging heads with cross-task inference refinement, a shared low-rank adapter trained on coverage-aware multi-source data with task-dependent decoding, and a training-free pipeline that maps captions to tags and applies detection test-time augmentation. The results show that a compact prompt-based vision-language model can serve all three outputs when supervision, decoding, and cross-task cues are designed for each task, while open-vocabulary detection on novel categories remains the main bottleneck.","authors":["Chang Liu","Henghui Ding","Tong Yang","Dayoung Kil","Seong-heum Kim","Qilin Xiang","Ronghao Zheng","Ziyang Zhang","Shanlin Jiang","Ruohan Shi","Qilin Fan","Chengfeng Qiu","Kaifeng Wei"],"published":"2026-11-12T15:14:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T15:14:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16925v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16925v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16925v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":36081,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：This report summarizes the Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding (MUMU) track of the 8th Large-scale Video Object Segmentation (LSVOS) Challenge, held in conjunction with ECCV 2026. MUMU asks for a single unified model that jointly performs multi-concept im...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16897v1","title":"TokenStream: Multi-Subject Personalized Video Generation with Token-wise LoRA and Guided Attention","abstract":"We introduce TokenStream, an optimization-based framework that composes independently learned subject identities in text-to-video generation without a reference-video training dataset or joint optimization of each new subject combination. Learning from a single reference image makes subject appearance difficult to separate from static-image bias, while merging independently trained adapters can mix identities. Token-wise LoRA (ToRA) restricts low-rank updates to the cross-attention value projections of subject-associated text tokens, providing a separate adaptation path for each subject. A T2V-guided stream copies queries and keys from the frozen base model during early denoising to reuse its layout and motion priors while retaining personalized values. We also introduce Multi-DB, a 30-subject, 122-prompt benchmark with appearance-free prompts and varied interactions. On Multi-DB at 1.3B, TokenStream improves identity preservation from 0.422 for DreamBooth to 0.467 and textual alignment from 0.652 to 0.688.","authors":["Hung Nguyen","Quang Qui-Vinh Nguyen","Khoi Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:54:14Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:54:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16897v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16897v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16897v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16869v1","title":"Raysemble: Metric 3D Bone Reconstruction from a Single X-ray","abstract":"Raysemble reconstructs eight lumbar and pelvic bones from a single X-ray by separating shape and placement. Each 3D query samples regional and global features at its image projection. A shared decoder predicts normalized bone shapes. Predicted depth and camera geometry determine 3D centers and scales for resampling these shapes onto a common grid in millimeters. Evaluation uses 23 held-out patients with 43 CT scans, each generating one simulated anteroposterior radiograph. Without rigid alignment to CT,Raysemble leads 15 evaluated methods, averaging 65.24% Dice, 9.09 mm HD95, and 3.32 mm ASSD over three random seeds. With identical inputs, it improves Dice over VolumeNeRF by 5.86 percentage points.","authors":["Xiaoyang Li","Xinghao Yan","Yuan Chai"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:38:06Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:38:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16869v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16869v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16869v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16861v1","title":"Visual Cipher Bench: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Long-Horizon Visual Reasoning","abstract":"While language-model agents excel at long-horizon coding and terminal tasks, this success relies on two conditions: explicit text specifications and immediate execution feedback. However, neither condition holds in real-world problems where critical information exists only in images, leaving it unclear whether long-horizon capability transfers from text to visual reasoning. In such visual settings, because rules and clues resist text transcription and lack test-case feedback, agents must repeatedly inspect images, simulate spatial transformations, and autonomously cross-check clues. Despite this challenge, existing evaluations fail to capture such capability: visual reasoning benchmarks largely reduce tasks to single-turn, short-chain question answering, whereas agent benchmarks focus on text-based environments or interactive states that permit trial-and-error. To bridge this gap, we introduce Visual Cipher Bench, a collection of 40 original visual puzzles across four categories: spatial manipulation and simulation, logical constraints and planning, rule induction, and knowledge-based decoding. Each puzzle provides only one to three black-and-white line drawings and a minimal submission instruction, requiring agents to discover the underlying solving mechanism entirely from the images. Across 29 reasoning-effort configurations of eight frontier models, even the strongest model, GPT-6 Astra (max), achieves only 26.7% accuracy, while five of the eight models score below 1%. Yet on a ten-task subset, providing a single non-spoiler mechanism hint boosts accuracy from 7.8% to 55.6%. This sharp contrast shows that the primary bottleneck of current agents lies not in executing multi-step plans, but in autonomously discovering rules from visual evidence. Visual Cipher Bench thus provides a rigorous benchmark for measuring long-horizon visual reasoning in agents.","authors":["Deyao Hong","Jiayi Jiang","Yuchun Chen","Yixuan Shi","Wenyi Li","Zheng Jiang","Chengxing Xie","Mingyu Su","Qingle Liu","Mingju Gao","Yizhe Chi","Youjie Zheng","Calvin Xiao","Kaisen Yang","Huan-ang Gao"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:33:46Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:33:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16861v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16861v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16861v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16840v1","title":"Kosmo-GFM: A Production-Ready Geometry Foundation Model","abstract":"We present Kosmo-GFM, a scene reconstruction system that integrates a geometry foundation model (GFM), a pose optimization engine (PoseEngine), and reconstruction-derived supervision from KosmoWorld. GFM predicts camera parameters, depth, cross-view correspondences, and dynamic 3D points from images and videos. PoseEngine combines these predictions with geometric optimization to recover scene-level camera poses and geometry for downstream 3D and 4D Gaussian Splatting. KosmoWorld generates images and geometric supervision from reconstructed real-world scenes to improve GFM's geometric predictions. On the Sparse and Medium subsets of SpatialBench, GFM achieves the lowest absolute relative depth error and the highest relative-pose AUC at 5 degrees among the compared methods. It also achieves the lowest mean endpoint errors for dynamic points and scene flow across the five evaluated datasets. Across 34 scenes completed by all compared reconstruction systems, PoseEngine achieves the highest scene-averaged pose AUC at thresholds of 1, 3, and 5 degrees, with a cumulative runtime of 14.07 hours, approximately 20% of GLUEMAP's 69.67 hours. PoseEngine Flash provides a further accuracy-efficiency trade-off, with a measured mixed-precision runtime of 2.62 hours, approximately 4% of GLUEMAP's runtime. Separate scaling experiments show generally favorable trends with increased data, model computation, and training budgets. These results support the use of learned geometric priors within a scene-level reconstruction pipeline.","authors":["Sheng Tang","Shengzhe Wei","Tiancheng Chen","Wenhua Jin","Xinyang Lu","Tinghao Jiang","Shuoxuan Feng","Yupeng Liu","Yu Zhang","Xingshuo Chang","Keren Zhou","Yuhan Xiong","Jiahao Li","Haoming Liu","Jituo Tian","Hui Liang","Chaoqun Tang","Feng Tian","Wentao Ouyang","Kaojin Zhu","Xiaolin Chai","Peihuang Fu","Miao Zheng","Yunbo Hu","Feixue Cai","Zizhen Zou","Weigang Wang","Yexuan Yang","Haoyu Feng","Weiqi Zhang","Juntong Fang","Zesong Li"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:17:22Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:17:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16840v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16840v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16840v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16767v1","title":"Evaluating Video LLMs' Understanding of Corner Cases in Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Natural language explanations promised by modern Video Large Language Models (Video LLMs) offer autonomous driving systems a path toward detailed scene description and interpretable decision making on the road. While recent benchmarks have begun assessing these models on driving scenes, they mainly target routine traffic conditions and general comprehension tasks based on existing driving datasets and therefore fail to evaluate model performance on the rarest and most safety-critical driving events, also known as \"corner cases\". To provide a comprehensive evaluation of Video LLMs on precisely these scenarios, we introduce DashBench, a benchmark testing 25 Video LLMs on 1,709 carefully curated driving videos spanning not only collision-related corner cases but also rare non-collision ones. Through extensive prompts and open-ended question answering, we expose a critical capability gap: all evaluated models perform substantially below the threshold required for safety-critical driving applications, frequently misjudging or missing dangerous events on the road ahead and hallucinating non-existent agents or actions in their explanations. We believe DashBench, combining an extensive dataset with a rigorous evaluation toolkit and a detailed diagnosis of current model limitations, drives progress toward safer, more explainable autonomous driving systems. The code and data will be shared at: cornercase-dashbench.github.io","authors":["Gabriel Manalu","Minh Son Hoang","Hyeontaek Hwang","Jaesung Lee","Hyojin Choi","Tran Tuan Phong","Seon Kwon Kim","Saad Wazir","Daeyoung Kim"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:15:27Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:15:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16767v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16767v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16767v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16752v1","title":"AgriSense: Vision-Language Decision Support and Robotic Intervention in a Virtual Greenhouse Copy title","abstract":"Autonomous greenhouse management increasingly requires robotic systems that integrate perception, navigation, environmental regulation, and plant-level intervention. This paper presents AgriSense, a framework combining an Isaac Sim virtual greenhouse, a quadruped mobile manipulator, RGB and LiDAR-inertial sensing, environmental control, and supervised vision-language decision support. Frozen DINOv2 and BERT encoders, together with structured environmental inputs, support joint prediction of twelve environmental references, a target zone, and an intervention flag. Checkpoint replay reproduces 41/43 zone decisions. Across five seeds on a source-grouped test set, the full, image-removed, and language-removed configurations each achieve 43/44 correct zone decisions, providing no evidence of incremental multimodal zone-accuracy improvement. The full model achieves mean absolute errors of 0.249 degrees C for temperature, 2.030 percentage points for humidity, and 0.997 percentage points for soil moisture. Sensor-only inputs match full-model zone accuracy, while language-only inputs achieve 68.64%. External real-image benchmarks reveal distribution-shift limitations. Five offline Point-LIO routes yield rigidly aligned trajectory errors of 5.22-17.32 mm. Of 40 virtual-arm targets, 32 satisfy geometric acceptance criteria and eight are rejected at boundaries. Six long-horizon climate cases and fault protocols characterize integration limits. Navigation and integrated treatment rely on simulator-state inputs; physical greenhouse performance and agronomic treatment efficacy remain unvalidated. The contribution is an evaluated decision-support and robotic-intervention framework for a virtual greenhouse.","authors":["Lyes Saad Saoud","Oualid Doukhi","Reza Ghorbani","Moussa Ayyash"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:04:57Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:04:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16752v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16752v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16752v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16749v1","title":"ORCA:Orthogonal Residual Consensus Alignment for Multi-View Clustering","abstract":"Deep multi-view clustering often suffers from redundant information accumulation during fusion and indiscriminate alignment across representation levels. We propose Orthogonal Residual Consensus Alignment (ORCA) to address these challenges. Its semantic incremental recurrent fusion module extracts cross-view orthogonal residuals as non-redundant increments and adaptively weights recurrent states through divergence-guided gating. Its consensus-calibrated hierarchical contrastive alignment module uses the global consensus to weight contrastive alignment of view-level, pairwise, and recurrent representations. Together, these modules balance view-specific structure with cross-view consistency. Experiments on five benchmarks against fifteen competing methods demonstrate ORCA's effectiveness, including a 5.25 percent accuracy improvement over the strongest baseline on 100leaves. Ablation studies validate both modules. Code is available at https://github.com/XiaojianDing/2026-NeurIPS-ORCA.","authors":["Xiaojian Ding","Xian Li","Xiaoying Zhu","Lin Zhao","Kaixiang Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:00:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:00:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16749v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16749v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16749v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16748v1","title":"PhysLoop: Harnessing LLMs' Physics Awareness for Self-Improving Video Generation Models","abstract":"Video generation models produce visually realistic videos but still struggle with physical dynamics and causal consistency. Learning from generated videos requires fine-grained physical supervision, yet reliable quality judgments, correction pairs, and controlled variations are difficult to obtain. Large language models (LLMs) possess physical knowledge, but struggle to reliably guide video generation models in producing their training videos. We present PhysLoop, a unified harness designed to support physically grounded self-improvement in video generation models. The harness guides and constrains LLMs throughout video generation, selection, and correction, distilling their physical knowledge into physically consistent videos. The framework supports controllable training-data construction and inference-time video repair. By identifying and correcting failures, PhysLoop marks a key milestone toward recursive self-improvement (RSI) in video generation models with limited human intervention. We release 100K videos spanning diverse, realistic indoor and outdoor scenes, along with generation tools and two evaluation datasets: PhysLoop-Gen and PhysLoop-Repair. We evaluate controllable generation and localized repair on these datasets, respectively, and assess physical behavior on the external Physics-IQ Verified benchmark.","authors":["Yuxuan Chen","Xiaojie Li","Hao Chen","Juncheng Ma","Kewei Zhang","Zhongjing Du","Guoqiang Wei","Fangfang Wang","Zhen Dong","Zhiyang Dou","Liming Zhao","Yan Zeng","Jianwei Zhang","Daquan Zhou"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:59:35Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:59:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16748v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16748v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16748v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16742v1","title":"Fog-FWL: A Large-Scale Full-Waveform LiDAR Dataset for Recovering Fog-Hidden Objects","abstract":"LiDAR is a core sensor for autonomous driving, yet it is highly vulnerable to fog. Fog scattering produces strong backscatter near the sensor and attenuates reflections from objects behind it, so weak object returns are often discarded by the peak detection that converts received signals into point clouds. Full-waveform LiDAR (FWL), which records the received optical signal before peak detection, retains these weak returns, but the lack of suitable data has prevented research on recovering fog-hidden objects from FWL. We present Fog-FWL, a dataset foundation for this task that consists of a real-world benchmark, Fog-FWL-Real, and a large-scale synthetic training set, Fog-FWL-Sim. Fog-FWL-Real contains 7,527 real foggy FWL frames from 8 scenes and 58 sequences, which is 579x larger than the only existing real foggy FWL data, together with histogram-level Object/Fog/Noise labels, 10,719 3D bounding boxes, and three difficulty levels defined by the attenuation of object reflections. Fog-FWL-Sim contains 12,000 frames generated by our physics-based simulator, Fog-FWL-Simulator, which integrates spatially heterogeneous fog density fields, time-resolved light transport including multiple scattering, and a photon-counting SPAD receiver model. The two subsets share the same sensor format and annotation protocol, enabling a strict sim-to-real evaluation in which no real waveforms are used for training. Our analysis shows that, under the hardest condition, object returns are included in the three strongest peaks for only 34.9% of object rays, underscoring the need for waveform-level recovery. Fog-FWL-Real, Fog-FWL-Sim, and the Fog-FWL-Simulator will be publicly released to facilitate research on fog-robust LiDAR perception.","authors":["Kazuma Ikeda","Kotaro Oishi","Ryosei Hara","Ryo Yoshida","Takahiro Kado","Ibuki Fujioka","Taro Beppu","Mariko Isogawa","Kentaro Yoshioka"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:56:55Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:56:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16742v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16742v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16742v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16727v1","title":"From Foundation VLM to Versatile Driving VLM: A Triplet Incremental Training Approach","abstract":"Adapting general Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to Autonomous Driving (AD) is thought to require heavy 3D spatial priors or intrusive architectural overhauls to compensate for a lack of spatial grounding. In this work, we present a counterintuitive pilot study: off-the-shelf general VLMs already exhibit remarkable adaptability across diverse driving tasks, while precise metric depth remains the primary missing link, a bottleneck readily addressable via onboard sensors and depth foundation models. To this end, we introduce the Triplet Incremental Training Framework, a non-intrusive paradigm that seamlessly instills 3D geometric awareness into a general VLM backbone while fully preserving its open-world capabilities. Driven by automatically constructed (image, depth, QA pair) triplets, our framework operates via a unified tri-stream training pipeline: an unfrozen primary VLM stream to adapt to driving tasks, an auxiliary depth stream that guides the visual backbone to implicitly imagine 3D scene geometry via explicit geometric constraints, and a frozen twin VLM stream employing logit-level KL distillation to prevent semantic drift. Crucially, the auxiliary depth branch is discarded during deployment, ensuring the enhanced geometric awareness comes with zero inference overhead. We evaluate our framework across four major benchmarks targeting four diverse capabilities spanning 3D spatial perception, driving-oriented language reasoning, end-to-end planning, and general multimodal understanding. Our approach outperforms state-of-the-art driving VLMs, rivals domain-specific expert models in 3D perception, and successfully retains its original general reasoning capabilities. This demonstrates that Triplet Incremental Training offers a unified, deployment-ready paradigm for transforming general foundation models into robust driving intelligences.","authors":["Shengtao Wu","Zichao Dong","Weixuan Qiao","Xiaolu Liu","Xin Zhan","Junbo Chen","Jianke Zhu"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:39:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:39:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16701v1","title":"Sprint3D: Accelerating End-to-End 3D Asset Generation through Pipeline Optimization","abstract":"Image-to-3D generative models now produce asset-quality textured meshes, but at minutes per asset they remain slow to iterate with and costly to deploy. Prior work has mostly accelerated the sampling of the networks, and where it also optimizes the mesh processing, it trains its own generators. We instead start from a publicly released pipeline and ask how much of its end-to-end time can be saved. Profiling the public Pixal3D pipeline shows that its latency is dominated by what happens after the networks: the four rectified flows take 14% of the wall-clock time and mesh post-processing takes 85%, most of it in CPU-based UV unwrapping. We therefore first rebuild the mesh post-processing on the GPU and take it off the critical path, then cut the per-evaluation overhead of the flows and distill them from 76 to 8 network evaluations. The resulting pipeline, Sprint3D, reduces the median time per asset from 133 s to 4.05 s on one H200 GPU at the same export settings (33$\\times$). At the same time, on 2,653 held-out Toys4K objects it improves every input-view normal metric of the Pixal3D protocol, with a slightly lower mean silhouette IoU, lower point-cloud alignment scores and less fine texture detail. The rebuilt post-processing alone also speeds up TRELLIS.2, which uses the same export code, by 6.7$\\times$ without a detectable change in the evaluated metrics.","authors":["Sho Kuno","Ruihan Yu","Yonghao Yu","Guixu Lin","Lian Fu","Muyao Niu","Yu-Ju Tsai","Zheng-Hui Huang","Yi-Chuan Huang","Siqi Yang","Fengbo Lan","Erwin Wu","Kaipeng Zhang","Zhixiang Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:18:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:18:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16701v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16701v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16701v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16655v1","title":"MuViRL: Multimodal View-Invariant Visual Speech Representation Learning via Calibrated View Aggregation","abstract":"Multimodal representation learning combines complementary signals to capture shared semantic information. For dynamic scenes, these representations need to remain consistent across views while preserving distinctions between temporal states. In visual speech representation learning, view variation changes the visibility and reliability of articulation cues, making it difficult to learn such representations from synchronized audio and video. To address this challenge, we propose MuViRL, a self-supervised framework for multimodal view-invariant visual speech representation learning via calibrated view aggregation. The framework combines synchronized 3D Gaussian view synthesis with multimodal self-supervision to learn consistent representations from real and synthesized data. A multimodal decision model learns relative view utility from teacher prediction losses, and its calibrated scores guide the aggregation of complementary information. Aggregation-to-Single Distillation transfers the aggregated predictions to a single branch. Real-synthetic alignment matches synchronized windows across views while preserving their temporal relations. Experiments on real and synthesized multiview data demonstrate that MuViRL improves multimodal representation consistency and visual speech recognition performance across views.","authors":["Fei Su","Kai Qiu","Juan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:31:59Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:31:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16655v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16655v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16655v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16639v1","title":"Asymmetric Multimodal Learning with Decomposed JEPAs","abstract":"Cross-modal alignment is the standard objective for building multimodal representations. We show that alignment as a \\emph{sole} objective is lossy wherever modalities are informationally asymmetric---where neither can express what the other carries. Visual question answering is an obvious case: a question is a selective relational operator probing a dense visual field whose fine-grained features no query recovers, so what alignment preserves is not what the task requires. We prove an \\textbf{Alignment Redundancy Bound}: alignment-only Dirichlet energy minimization retains at most the redundant information atom $\\Red(Z; X_1, X_2)$, annihilating private modality-specific structure $\\Unq(Z; X_m)$ and cross-modal synergy $\\Syn(Z; X_1, X_2)$. We introduce \\textbf{Decomposed JEPA (D-JEPA)}, which factorizes latents into an aligned shared core and private modality-specific fibers. The shared core retains most of the structure alignment targets; the private fibers recover what alignment discards, via contextual masked prediction and asymmetric cross-subspace independence. Under information asymmetry, alignment-only training undergoes catastrophic semantic collapse on compositional reasoning benchmarks, surviving only on binary queries. D-JEPA averts this collapse, substantially improving downstream accuracy over both alignment-only and alignment-with-reconstruction baselines, and restoring a positive multimodal synergy gap in place of the baseline's inverted gap. Information-theoretic audits confirm that D-JEPA recovers most of the mutual information available from a frozen backbone, and causal fiber-swap interventions demonstrate that the recovered information is causally localized in the private fibers.","authors":["Yusuf Shihata"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:13:13Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:13:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16639v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16639v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16639v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16630v1","title":"When Chekhov's Gun Fires Anyway: Over-Realization in Text-to-Image Models","abstract":"In text-to-image generation, a target that the prompt mentions often appears in the generated image in a way the prompt does not call for. We call this failure over-realization. Mental-spaces theory points to the situations where it can happen: under a negation, inside what a character believes or imagines, as the vehicle of a figurative comparison, or behind a viewpoint that hides the target. Prior work has studied only negation in depth, and the conventional alignment and preference metrics do not penalize the failure. We build OverReal to measure it: OverReal-Gen, 698 prompts covering the four situations, on which we measure each generator's over-realization rate; and OverReal-Det, 2,841 generated images human-labeled for whether and how the target is over-realized, on which we verify the automatic judge that produces this rate. Our evaluation of eight text-to-image systems reveals that all of them over-realize, and that conventional metrics such as CLIPScore reward the failure rather than penalize it. Prompt expansion only partly mitigates the failure. Inside the generator, the diffusion backbone attends to a mentioned target almost as much as to a requested one. The cue words that should hold the target back receive less attention than ordinary words.","authors":["Jiahao Huang","Kon Woo Kim","Song-Duo Ma","Yuka Kitamura","Felix Wagner","Yunshan Huang","Sunisth Kumar","Xanh Ho","Yun-Ang Wu","Zhijie Chen","Yunjia Xu","Junfeng Jiang","Zefan Yu","Fei Cheng","Akiko Aizawa"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:03:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:03:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16630v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16630v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16630v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16608v1","title":"Towards Democratising Low-Data Medical Image Classification: CPU-Trainable ZACH-ViT and Its Geometric Variants","abstract":"Medical image classification in resource-constrained settings must contend with limited annotations and computational capacity. We evaluate four ZACH-ViT variants with linear, Euclidean prototype, Poincare and spherical heads alongside two compact multiple-instance-learning baselines. All models are trained from scratch with fewer than 260,000 parameters across seven MedMNIST+ datasets and three external benchmarks (PCam, ISIC2017 and Shenzhen tuberculosis radiographs). The low-data protocol uses 50 training images per class and five seeds, with validation-selected checkpoints at 23 and 47 epochs; a full-data reference uses one seed. Compact-TransMIL achieves the highest ten-dataset mean test Macro-F1 at 23 epochs (53.34%) and with full data (73.93%). At 47 epochs, ZACH-Linear narrowly leads (57.76% versus 57.54% for Compact-TransMIL). Extending training improves Macro-F1 in 239 of 300 paired comparisons. On ISIC2017, hZACH-Spherical has the highest 47-epoch low-data Macro-F1 (44.09% with SD 4.09%), whereas ZACH-EuclideanProto leads with full data (51.27%). We additionally conduct 40 CPU-only training runs on ISIC2017 and Shenzhen using four CPU threads; model-only single-image inference requires approximately 4-9 ms, while image decoding and preprocessing substantially increase end-to-end latency. The results demonstrate the feasibility of CPU-only training on the tested platform and the task- and regime-dependence of compact medical imaging under strict resource constraints.","authors":["Athanasios Angelakis"],"published":"2026-11-12T10:46:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T10:46:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16579v1","title":"ActWorld-JEPA: Action-Conditioned JEPA World Models Meet Latent Planning for End-to-End Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) enable future prediction in latent space without pixel reconstruction. However, latent predictive consistency alone does not establish the relative driving quality of candidate plans. We present ActWorld-JEPA, a joint-embedding formulation for candidate-based driving. Where Plan-Conditioned JEPA (PC-JEPA) uses global and step-wise trajectory intentions to guide recurrent latent prediction, then combines the predicted future with the current scene through gated cross-attention; Relational Residual Reranking ($R^3$) models agreement and discrepancy between plan and future representations under planning supervision, yielding bounded, gated residual corrections to the base planning scores. This formulation connects latent prediction to candidate selection using only observed context and proposed trajectories at inference. Benchmark-specific implementations report 94.1 PDMS on NAVSIM v1 navtest, with the final selector calibrated using aggregate NAVTEST feedback, and 91.6 and 42.7 EPDMS on NAVSIM v2 navtest and navhard, respectively. The nuScenes evaluation reports a mean $L_2$ error of 1.20 m and a predicted collision rate of 0.76% over 1--6 s. This work provides an effective path from JEPA's predictive representations to driving decisions informed by anticipated consequences.","authors":["Ziying Song","Shengkai Zhang","Caiyan Jia","Lei Yang","Lin Liu","Yuchen Liu","Ziyang Liu","Zirui Li","Gang Wang","Peiliang Wu","Li Wang","Yadan Luo","Chen Lv"],"published":"2026-11-12T10:10:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T10:10:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16579v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16579v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16579v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16534v1","title":"Context-Predictive Low-Rank Adaptation for Face Presentation Attack Detection","abstract":"Face presentation attack detection requires representations that distinguish physical spoofing cues across changes in acquisition devices, subjects, and attack media. Large visual encoders offer transferable representations, but conventional binary fine-tuning supervises their final decisions without explicitly constraining how local facial evidence is represented under partial observation. We present CP-JEPA, a context-predictive adaptation framework that combines low-rank tuning of a pretrained CLIP image encoder with a joint-embedding predictive objective. A student predicts complete-image teacher features at masked spatial locations, while unmasked classification, masked classification, and prediction consistency retain the connection to the live/spoof task. An exponential-moving-average teacher supplies evolving latent targets. The auxiliary components are used only during training; inference requires only the LoRA-adapted CLIP visual encoder and binary classifier. {We evaluate CP-JEPA under single-source and multi-source OCIM transfer, additional CelebA-Spoof training, transfer to CelebA-Spoof, two-source training, and unseen-attack evaluation on SiW-Mv2.} On the standard OCIM benchmark, CP-JEPA achieves 3.74\\% mean HTER and 98.73\\% mean AUC. {Single-source evaluation achieves 11.36\\% mean HTER across twelve source--target combinations.} With CelebA-Spoof added to the three OCIM training sources, CP-JEPA reduces mean HTER from 3.45\\% to 2.35\\% and increases mean AUC from 98.68\\% to 99.32\\%. On SiW-Mv2, it achieves 3.36\\% mean HTER across the held-out attack categories. Controlled loss ablations examine the contributions of latent prediction, masked classification, and prediction consistency. The results show that context-predictive adaptation can improve cross-dataset PAD performance, although the gains vary with the training sources and evaluation setting.","authors":["Aminu Yusuf","Shakeel Muhammad Ibrahim","Sulaimon Oyeniyi Adebayo","Muhammad Sohail Ibrahim","Muhammad Shahid Jabbar","Shujaat Khan"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:32:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:32:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16534v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16534v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16534v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16517v1","title":"UniSpark: Exploring Hybrid Attention for Multimodal Understanding and Generation","abstract":"We present UniSpark-9B, a unified multimodal model that investigates hybrid attention for visual understanding and generation. Built on a Mixture-of-Transformer-Experts backbone with approximately 9B parameters in each branch, UniSpark combines full attention for explicit token interactions with GatedDeltaNet linear attention for compact context propagation. Autoregressive text prediction and visual flow matching jointly support visual question answering, text-to-image generation, instruction-guided editing, and interleaved text-image prediction. In interleaved generation, noisy visual targets are isolated from subsequent predictions, while clean visual representations provide context for later outputs. A blockwise protocol enforces this distinction through attention masks in full-attention layers and recurrent-state retention rules in linear-attention layers. The selected training sources contain 159.7M generation samples, 213.4M understanding samples, and 624.8K interleaved sequences. A four-stage curriculum jointly trains multimodal understanding and visual generation, refining answer accuracy and instruction following while extending image synthesis to 2K. UniSpark scores 2470.3 on MME-S and 0.83 on GenEval. Qualitative examples further illustrate its capabilities in interleaved image generation, video synthesis, and video editing. Together, these results demonstrate that hybrid attention provides an effective backbone for unified multimodal understanding and generation.","authors":["Jiancheng Pan","Yuzheng Liu","Hengyuan Cao","Qiyuan Zhang","Yangyan Li","Biao Gong","Zheng Zhang","Ka Long Cheng","Xinyang Wang","Yujun Shen"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:16:10Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:16:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16517v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16517v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16517v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16515v1","title":"PiLLVD: A Real-World Low-Light Video Dataset for Dynamic Scenes Captured with Off-the-Shelf Cameras","abstract":"Low-light image and video enhancement has received increasing attention in recent years. However, most existing enhancement methods are developed and evaluated on still images, while progress in low-light video enhancement remains limited by the lack of real-world paired video datasets. Acquiring paired low-light and normal-light video sequences is challenging because it requires spatially and temporally aligned captures while avoiding motion blur, exposure mismatch, and correspondence errors in dynamic scenes. To address this gap, we introduce PiLLVD, the Pi-based Low-Light Video Dataset, a paired low-light video dataset captured using an accessible Raspberry Pi-based multi-camera system with off-the-shelf cameras. PiLLVD provides paired low-light and aligned normal-light reference sequences for dynamic indoor and outdoor scenes, including diverse human and object motion under varying illumination conditions. Unlike existing datasets that are often image-based, static, synthetic, or limited to simple motion patterns, PiLLVD focuses on motion-rich scenes where frame-to-frame stability and motion-related artefacts are important for evaluation. In addition to presenting the dataset and acquisition pipeline, we benchmark representative pre-trained low-light video enhancement methods on PiLLVD under a zero-shot setting. The results show that existing methods do not generalise uniformly across the proposed sequences, highlighting the challenges posed by the proposed dataset and its value for both benchmarking existing methods and supporting the development of future low-light video enhancement algorithms.","authors":["Yaser Abu Awwad","Paul L. Rosin","Oktay Karakuş"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:13:12Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:13:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16515v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16515v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16515v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16452v1","title":"Can an LVLM Perceive Event Boundaries Like a Human?","abstract":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) reason effectively about video content, but whether they perceive its event boundaries in a manner consistent with humans remains unclear. We first investigate this through Generic Event Boundary Detection (GEBD), which identifies event boundaries naturally perceived by humans in continuous video, without relying on predefined event categories. On Kinetics-GEBD dataset, LVLMs miss many boundaries when predicting directly from frames, but effectively refine candidates proposed by an unsupervised discovery method, achieving state-of-the-art unsupervised GEBD performance. We extend this discovery-refinement approach to hour-long egocentric video, where boundary discovery requires a broader semantic scale and whole-video LVLM refinement becomes impractical. We address these challenges through coarse-to-fine temporal discovery and localized LVLM accept/reject judgments. Human evaluation shows that the LVLM's agreement with group consensus falls within the range observed among human annotators, with disagreements concentrated on low-consensus cases. Using this pipeline, we construct EgoBoundary, a benchmark for event boundary detection in hour-scale egocentric video. Representative supervised and unsupervised methods degrade substantially on EgoBoundary, highlighting the challenge of long-horizon event boundary detection.","authors":["Hyolim Kang","Jungsoo Lee","Yeji Song","Whie Jung","Janghoon Cho","Juntae Lee"],"published":"2026-11-12T08:06:48Z","updated":"2026-11-12T08:06:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16452v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16452v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16452v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16451v1","title":"HIFC-IQA: Train-Free Cross-Domain Image Quality Assessment via Dual-Process Cognition","abstract":"The gap between controlled synthetic distortions and complex real-world degradations introduces a critical domain shift challenge for No-Reference Image Quality Assessment. Although Unsupervised Domain Adaptation (UDA) approaches attempt to address this issue, they remain constrained by substantial retraining overhead and cumulative error propagation inherent in iterative pseudo-labeling. Inspired by the dual-process theory of human cognition for remarkable generalization ability, we propose Hierarchical Intuitive and Fuzzy Consensus (HIFC), a completely train-free Cross-Domain IQA framework. HIFC employs asymmetric perceptual decomposition to disentangle semantic content from distortion-sensitive representations. The final quality score is fused through the synergy of two complementary branches: a memory consensus branch that retrieves a stable Fuzzy Quality Centroid from a synthetic reference gallery, and an intuitive branch that captures aesthetic polarization via zero-shot vision-language alignment. Extensive experiments show that HIFC achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple benchmarks, attaining the highest average SRCC and PLCC among competing UDA methods. Most notably, in the challenging synthetic-to-authentic cross-domain setting, HIFC achieves a substantial performance margin over competing methods, demonstrating remarkable robustness and generalization.","authors":["Yu Li","Zhengran Shen","Puchao Zhou","Yukang Ding","Yachun Mi","Boyuan Yang","Xi Zhai","Shaohui Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T08:04:40Z","updated":"2026-11-12T08:04:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16451v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16451v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16451v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16424v1","title":"PhysCapBench: A Cross-Domain Benchmark for Physically Grounded Video Captioning","abstract":"Physical AI aims to learn transferable physical knowledge from real-world experience to understand, predict, and act in the physical world. Internet videos and embodied demonstrations provide complementary observations of motion, interaction, and state evolution. A foundational data challenge is to measure and preserve this physical information as training supervision. In video-language pretraining, captions express observed processes in language, yet a semantically correct description can omit the interactions and transitions that define those processes. Rich physical experience in video does not automatically translate into sufficient language supervision. This gap calls for a yardstick to assess annotation quality, compare captioning strategies, and guide training-data refinement. Motivated by our data construction for world-model pretraining, we introduce PhysCapBench, to our knowledge the first unified physical-caption benchmark spanning all three domains: third-person scenes, egocentric manipulation, and handheld-gripper demonstrations. It provides human-verified annotations for 1,500 video sequences and evaluates accuracy and coverage through seven physical dimensions. We examine downstream utility through video reconstruction and manipulation-policy fine-tuning. Physical captions reduce reconstruction LPIPS by 8.6-9.5% on third-person videos and 26.9-30.9% on egocentric videos relative to metadata captions. Training-instruction enrichment improves both PhysCapBench scores and policy success, including a 7.5-percentage-point gain for pi0.5 on a RoboTwin 2.0 subtask. PhysCapBench makes the physical content of language supervision inspectable and comparable, providing a cross-domain basis for its evaluation and improvement.","authors":["Haofeng Wang","Pengcheng Wen","Ruiqi Li","Yuhao Zhang","Tiange Zhang","Boren Zheng","Xuanyi Liu","Siyu Tao","Danni Yu","Gan Lin","Haocheng Tang","Tianzizhe Guo","Mingyuan Su","Boyuan Chen","Zhongyuan Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:33:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:33:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16424v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16424v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16424v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16418v1","title":"Model Distribution-Aware Multimodal Dataset Distillation","abstract":"Multimodal dataset distillation (MDD) aims to reduce the cost of vision-language pre-training by synthesizing compact image-text datasets. Although recent feature-matching methods have improved distillation efficiency, synthetic data may become specialized to the model representations encountered during optimization, limiting generalization to unseen model states. We propose Model Distribution-Aware Multimodal Dataset Distillation (MDA-DD), which leverages supervision from a dynamic staggered queue of models at different training stages. We further introduce asymmetric dual projection for complementary alignment in visual and linguistic spaces and an uncentered second-order moment objective that retains mean-related cross-modal information. Experiments on Flickr30K and MS-COCO demonstrate consistent retrieval improvements over existing methods across synthetic-data budgets. With only 100 synthetic pairs, MDA-DD improves IR@1 and TR@1 on Flickr30K by 12.9 and 18.2 percentage points, respectively, over the strongest baseline at the same budget, outperforming all compared methods using 500 pairs on both datasets across all six retrieval metrics.","authors":["Jiajun Shen","Yonglin Wu","Ruonan Yu","Xinchao Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:29:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:29:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16418v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16418v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16418v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16417v1","title":"VBot Exo2Ego Policy: Lightweight Data Collection and Exocentric-to-Egocentric Video Generation","abstract":"We present VBot Exo2Ego Policy, a video generation model that turns third-person videos into first-person observations of a selected actor, offering a practical way to expand first-person operation data from abundant real-world footage. To support this goal, we introduce a lightweight data collection pipeline and mobile app that support first-person recording, third-person recording, and synchronized capture of both views. This setup combines accessible single-view collection with paired RGB and camera geometry for learning cross-view correspondence. Building on a pretrained video diffusion transformer, our model incorporates geometric guidance and auxiliary supervision from semantic masks and hand keypoints to facilitate optimization and improve the fidelity of hand-object interactions. Camera geometry is injected stochastically during training and gradually removed, allowing inference without target-camera calibration. To accommodate the diversity of Internet videos, we combine source-video perception and reasoning, optional actor references, and multi-shot adaptation to identify the intended actor and follow an operation across camera cuts. The collection system and generation model provide a foundation for expanding first-person operation data across tasks, scenes, and participants.","authors":["Tong Rao","Zhihao Zhang","Yuntian Wang","Siyu Zhou","Hailong Qin","Yinan Yu"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:27:47Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:27:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16417v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16417v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16417v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16413v1","title":"RICapBench: A Fine-Grained Visual Understanding Benchmark Dataset for Vision-Language Models in Remote Sensing","abstract":"Advances in vision-language models (VLMs) have improved remote sensing image analysis and supported geospatial decision-making. However, fine-grained understanding of remote sensing imagery remains challenging for VLMs. Large variations in object scale, complex geospatial relations among instances, and limited visual detail hinder precise instance localization, visible attribute identification, and spatial relation understanding, potentially leading to descriptions that lack visual grounding. Existing datasets and benchmarks insufficiently assess end-to-end instance localization and captioning without target-specific textual cues. To support this evaluation, we introduce RICapBench, a dataset and benchmark that unifies instance localization and fine-grained captioning in remote sensing. RICapBench contains 245,466 instance-level annotations and 498,005 spatial relation tuples spanning general remote sensing, UAV remote sensing, and dense small-object scenarios. Each annotation pairs an object's oriented bounding box with a natural language description of its visible attributes and spatial relations to surrounding instances and environmental elements. To evaluate the accuracy and completeness of spatial relation captioning, we propose a remote sensing spatial relation parser and the RelCap-F1 metric. We combine RelCap-F1 with instance localization and attribute captioning metrics into a composite metric, RICScore. Our evaluation of 15 advanced open-source and closed-source VLMs identifies instance localization as a major bottleneck for these models. LocateAnything achieves the highest RICScore of 31.68, but scores lower on spatial relation captioning than other task-fine-tuned open-source VLMs. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/Xldln/RICapBench.","authors":["Xue Wang","Yubin Xu","Kun Tan"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:24:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:24:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16413v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16413v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16413v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16402v1","title":"Teach with Blinks: Breaking Linguistic Momentum in On-Policy Distillation for Fine-Grained Multimodal Perception","abstract":"On-policy distillation (OPD) has emerged as an effective post-training approach to improving fine-grained perception in multimodal large language models by amortizing costly inference-time zooming operations into a single-pass recipe. However, vanilla OPD is inherently susceptible to linguistic momentum: the teacher can follow the student's erroneous prefixes, thus weakening corrective supervision. To address this, we propose Blink On-Policy Distillation (BlinkOPD), in which the teacher \"blinks\" at every token: it reads the same prefix once with its eyes open and once with its eyes closed. A first-glance gain further strengthens the correction on rollouts that conflict with the teacher. By extracting more effective supervision from each on-policy trajectory, BlinkOPD achieves state-of-the-art average performance across six fine-grained benchmarks using only 500 training images (12x less data) than existing OPD methods. Beyond fine-grained perception, BlinkOPD also demonstrates strong zero-shot improvements across ten held-out benchmarks spanning general visual perception, visual hallucination, and multimodal reasoning abilities. Code: https://github.com/Supltz/BlinkOPD","authors":["Tangzheng Lian","Hang Zhang","Guanyu Hu","Renjiu Hu","Zhensong Zhang","Jiankang Deng","Jifei Song"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:11:20Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:11:20Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16402v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16402v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16402v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16400v1","title":"FuGIBench: Evaluating Reliable Preparation in Streaming Video","abstract":"An assistant watching a worker can prepare for an emerging need before the worker is ready to receive help.As the need evolves, the assistant must maintain preparation until a response is due, or withdraw it if the need disappears. Video anticipation and interactive response benchmarks leave this interval between early evidence and response largely unmeasured. We introduce FuGIBench (Future-Grounded Interaction), a streaming-video benchmark in which a model chooses among waiting, reversible preparation, and response given a causal video prefix and a task contract. The benchmark contains 19,893 synthetic tool-assistance videos across ten tool tasks, in handover, ordinary-activity, and cancelled-need tracks. One primary score credits sustained preparation, incorporates response-interval alignment, and penalizes activity when the reference prescribes waiting. On a frozen set of 1,000 episodes, a reference system with a longer preparation lead has poorer response alignment, while similar handover leads accompany different levels of activity during ordinary work in matched scenarios. Together with cancellation analyses, these results show why evaluating preparation requires tracking decisions as needs develop, become actionable, or disappear.","authors":["Changheng Cai","Zhichao Huang","Song Ren","Zhichen Zhao","Hongsen Liu","Chaoyang Zheng","Wenxi Wu","Yuan Fang","Wei Wang","Yang Wang","Yuchen Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:10:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:10:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16400v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16400v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16400v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16373v1","title":"Description-Guided CLIP Adaptation with Local Evidence Calibration for Few-Shot Plant Disease Recognition","abstract":"Accurate plant disease recognition is essential for intelligent agriculture, but few-shot settings provide limited labeled samples that may not capture diagnostic symptoms and intra-class variations. Pretrained vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, offer transferable visual-semantic knowledge. However, existing CLIP-based methods often rely on coarse class semantics and global visual representations, overlooking disease-specific diagnostic information and fine-grained local cues. Excessive few-shot adaptation may also cause over-specialization and weaken the generalization ability of large-scale pre-training. We propose a description-guided, two-stage CLIP adaptation method to address these issues. Symptom and comparative descriptions enrich class semantics with typical visual symptoms and discriminative cues between similar diseases. The enhanced semantics guide the aggregation of class-relevant patch evidence to calibrate global image-text matching with fine-grained cues. For adaptation, we first update the vision-language encoders with lightweight parameters, then freeze them and train a linear classifier. At inference, the classifier is used for in-domain prediction, while class prototypes from the adapted text encoder support cross-domain prediction and retain vision-language transferability. Experiments with two backbones show that our method achieves the highest mean accuracy in most few-shot settings on PlantVillage, Cassava, and Pear, reaching 93.16%, 79.30%, and 96.56%, respectively, with ViT-L/14 under 16-shot. On class-aligned cross-domain evaluation across 21 shared PlantDoc classes, it achieves 56.96% accuracy, outperforming all compared methods. These results demonstrate its effectiveness in fine-grained few-shot plant disease recognition and cross-domain generalization.","authors":["Zhenkun Ye","Qian Zhou","Hua Zou"],"published":"2026-11-12T06:40:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T06:40:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16373v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16373v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16373v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16339v1","title":"Hand in Silhouette: Synthetic-to-Real Multi-View Hand Reconstruction via Binary Masks","abstract":"Calibrated multi-view hand reconstruction has advanced rapidly, yet the effectiveness of existing methods depends strongly on real captured data, which is costly to acquire, limited in hand-geometry diversity, and often affected by imperfect annotations. Synthetic data offers a scalable alternative with accurate supervision, but a substantial synthetic-to-real appearance gap hinders its use. To address this, we introduce Hand in Silhouette (SilHand), a calibrated multi-view hand reconstruction framework that operates on binary hand masks, obtainable at low cost either automatically or manually, to reduce the synthetic-to-real observation gap. Rather than relying on RGB features for 3D lifting, SilHand projects metric 3D hypotheses into each view to query and fuse mask-ray evidence for hand reconstruction. This mask-centric formulation enables training on a large-scale synthetic dataset comprising 46.8k grasping poses and 60k free-hand samples, providing broad coverage of hand geometry while directly transferring to real captured hand geometry. Across five real-world benchmarks, SilHand improves relative reconstruction accuracy from 49.2% to 63.4% using masks rendered from reference hands. Moreover, reconstruction accuracy improves consistently as the synthetic training set grows, indicating that broader geometric coverage translates into better real-world performance. While achieving such diversity is difficult with real captured data, it becomes practical under our mask-centric formulation. Finally, SilHand remains robust under mask corruption, with reconstruction error increasing by at most 1.63 mm. Together, these results support binary masks as an effective interface for learning hand reconstruction from synthetic geometry.","authors":["Leiyao Cui","Lecheng Ruan","Zihang Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:59:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:59:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16339v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16339v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16339v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16310v1","title":"Selective Generative Restoration for Video Deraining via Uncertainty-Aware Diffusion","abstract":"Video deraining is challenging due to rain streaks, occlusions, and atmospheric effects. Existing methods mainly follow a deterministic restoration paradigm, assuming that clean scenes can be uniquely recovered from rainy observations. However, rain degradation is spatially heterogeneous. Reliable regions require faithful reconstruction, whereas severely occluded regions contain ambiguous content that require stronger prior-driven inference. Therefore, conventional deterministic restoration often produces over-smoothed results, while global generative restoration may introduce unnecessary hallucinations. We introduce a Selective Generative Restoration paradigm for video deraining, where generative priors are adaptively activated according to restoration uncertainty. Based on this principle, we propose Selective Generative Video Deraining (SGVD), an uncertainty-aware diffusion framework that enables adaptive fidelity preservation and generative recovery. Specifically, SGVD exploits stochastic diversity between dual one-step diffusion predictions to estimate region-level restoration uncertainty, which determines where observation fidelity should be preserved and where generative inference should be activated. Furthermore, an anchor-guided trajectory consistency mechanism is proposed to regularize stochastic diffusion evolution across frames, reducing temporal generation drift and improving video coherence. To achieve practical efficiency, SGVD adopts one-step diffusion inference while maintaining uncertainty-aware generative capability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SGVD achieves superior restoration performance, perceptual quality, and temporal consistency compared with state-of-the-art video deraining methods.","authors":["Yi'ang Chen","Han Li","Laibin Chang","Hui Liu","Kui Jiang","Junjun Jiang"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:10:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:10:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16310v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16310v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16310v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16307v1","title":"DEDCA: Test-Time Adaptation for Generalized AI-Generated Image Detection","abstract":"AI-generated image detectors face significant challenges when deployed in real-world environments, particularly when test samples are produced by unseen generators that deviate from the source training distribution. Existing detectors often rely on a single type of evidence, such as semantic representations or low-level generation traces, making them vulnerable when the corresponding cue becomes unreliable. Moreover, adapting detectors to newly emerging generators usually requires target labels or retraining, which is costly and impractical. To address these challenges, we propose Dual-Evidence Disagreement-Constrained Adaptation (DEDCA), a test-time adaptation framework for generalized AI-generated image detection. DEDCA constructs a dual-evidence detector by combining CLIP-based semantic evidence with trace evidence extracted from shuffled entropy and residual statistics, and introduces a reliability-aware gate to perform sample-specific adaptive fusion. Our key idea is to exploit the disagreement between the two evidence streams as an unlabeled adaptation signal, enabling the detector to adjust to target-domain shifts without relying on target labels or blindly trusting a single prediction stream. Experiments show that DEDCA achieves superior performance on the GenImage benchmark by outperforming state-of-the-art AI-generated image detectors and test-time adaptation methods with 92.0\\% average accuracy.","authors":["Hao Tan","Qimin Zhang","Lihuang Fang","Kebing Jin","Jinghui Qin"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:07:23Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:07:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16307v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16307v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16307v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16269v1","title":"FlowIR: Depth-driven Rectified Flow for Resolving Thermal Ambiguity in Infrared Super-Resolution","abstract":"Distinguishing different heat sources in low-resolution infrared (IR) images is inherently difficult, as multiple thermal emitters often collapse into ambiguous and overlapping responses. Existing super-resolution methods hallucinate structures from such ambiguous 2D evidence, leading to thermal drift, boundary merging, and unreliable restoration under infrared degradations. We propose FlowIR, a depth-driven rectified flow framework that resolves thermal ambiguity by injecting 3D geometric cues into the generation process. Pseudo-3D depth lifting and depth-guided 3D convolutions disentangle overlapping foreground objects, while cluster-wise inter-attention suppresses cross-source feature contamination. A Thermal-Geometry Consistency Loss further enforces geometric alignment during refinement. Built upon a lightweight rectified flow backbone, FlowIR restores IR images in only a few steps, producing sharply separated, degradation-robust, and geometrically faithful high-resolution results.","authors":["Xingyue Zhu","Qinyun Mei","Zhexi Min","Yang Zou","Xingyuan Li","Zhiying Jiang","Jinyuan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T04:15:32Z","updated":"2026-11-12T04:15:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16269v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16269v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16269v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16259v1","title":"Reconstruct What You Can: Distractor-free 4D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Dynamic reconstruction from casual video must preserve valid subject motion while suppressing transient occluders, shadows, and volumetric effects. Existing methods either suppress valid motion through static-scene assumptions or model all observations jointly, producing ghosting and unstable geometry. We introduce ReCan-GS, a 4D Gaussian Splatting framework that separates coherent scene content from inconsistent observations. It derives a Jacobian-based deformation-sensitivity score from the learned deformation field, using it as an empirical proxy for reconstruction ambiguity. The score guides separation into dynamic Core Gaussians and per-view Distractor Gaussians, or post-hoc pruning of sensitive Core Gaussians. The same criterion supports removal of spatial occluders, photometric artifacts, and evolving smoke or fog without masks, pretrained segmenters, or medium-specific supervision. Experiments on synthetic and real dynamic scenes and synthetic smoke/fog scenes demonstrate its usefulness across the evaluated distractor regimes.","authors":["Joel Julin","Liuyue Xie","Ananya Bal","Mose Sakashita","Koichiro Niinuma","Laszlo A. Jeni"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:59:45Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:59:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16259v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16259v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16259v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16224v1","title":"Disentangled Embedding Optimization for Diffusion-Based Dataset Distillation","abstract":"Dataset distillation aims to compress large-scale datasets into compact synthetic subsets that enable efficient model training with comparable performance to the original data. Recent advances show that diffusion models are highly effective for this task due to their strong generative capability. Existing diffusion-based approaches typically perform class-wise clustering and use the obtained cluster prototypes as diffusion inputs to generate distilled samples. However, the uniformly distributed original samples in a category inevitably lead to entanglement near cluster boundaries, pulling the cluster prototypes closer and reducing the inter-cluster separation. Consequently, the diffusion model receives overly similar latent inputs, resulting in synthetic data with limited diversity. To address this issue, we propose Disentangled Embedding Optimization (DEO), a simple yet effective framework that replaces static clustering with trainable embeddings. These embeddings are optimized to match the Gaussian statistics (i.e., mean and covariance) of the original data distribution, while a diversity regularizer is designed to encourage separation among these embeddings to improve the intra-class variability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our proposed DEO can generate more diversified and representative distilled data than existing cluster-based diffusion methods, achieving state-of-the-art performance and superior generalization across multiple datasets.","authors":["Hongcheng Li","Yucan Zhou","Yibin Wang","Xiaoyan Gu","Bo Li","Weiping Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:18:19Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:18:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16224v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16224v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16224v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16217v1","title":"WorldDraft: If Code Builds the World, Can Video Models Just Render It?","abstract":"Multimodal coding agents decouple 4D world construction from visual generation, repurposing video models from world generators toward generative renderers. This division of labor raises a fundamental question: if code builds the world, can video models just render it? A reliable generative renderer hinges on two complementary criteria: (i) world preservation, retaining prescribed 4D kinematics and camera paths; and (ii) visual realization, persistently binding text-specified target attributes over time. To evaluate this capability, we introduce WorldDraft, a benchmark of $432$ strictly paired test cases across $48$ executable 4D world families spanning six dynamic challenge categories. Under a $3 \\times 3$ factorial design, WorldDraft orthogonally crosses draft granularity (abstract proxy to previs) and appearance alignment (neutral to conflicting) under identical dynamics. Extensive evaluations across $12$ representative models reveal that: (I) world preservation and visual realization remain in stark operational tension; (II) richer draft scaffolding anchors structure but paradoxically impairs appearance fidelity; and (III) conflicting source cues trigger severe source leakage (up to $64\\%$), exposing fragile cross-modal disentanglement. To mitigate these bottlenecks, we propose WorldDraft-Primer, a plug-and-play visual prompting strategy that consistently improves both objectives across frozen video renderers. Together, WorldDraft and WorldDraft-Primer lay a foundation for bridging programmatic 4D simulation with video generation, advancing physically grounded generative rendering.","authors":["Zhiyu Hou","Wen-Jie Shu","Harold Haodong Chen","Weilin Ruan","Chenfei Liao","Liuzhou Zhang","Disen Lan","Xiaogang Xu","Qi Chen","Ser-Nam Lim"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:09:18Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:09:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16217v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16217v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16217v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16212v1","title":"SPEM: Scan-Prior Entropy Modeling for LiDAR Point Cloud Compression","abstract":"LiDAR point clouds exhibit scan-induced spatial regularities that are informative for geometry compression. Existing scan-aware approaches often exploit these regularities by transforming coded geometry into spherical or beam-aligned coordinate systems. However, this couples acquisition priors with geometry representation, altering spatial partitioning and quantization, and potentially preventing exact recovery of the original Cartesian geometry. We argue that acquisition priors should guide probability modeling rather than dictate the coding representation. We propose SPEM, a Scan-Prior Entropy Modeling framework that exploits LiDAR scanning regularities within the entropy model without requiring coordinate transformation. We introduce a multi-frequency spherical rotary positional encoding that captures scan-aligned spatial relationships in both backbone self-attention and decoded-context retrieval. Building upon this encoding, we develop a decoded-memory autoregressive predictor that efficiently exploits previously decoded occupancy information through reusable key/value caches. Experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art performance in both lossy and lossless LiDAR geometry compression, highlighting the effectiveness of scan-prior modeling across different coordinate representations.","authors":["Bojun Liu","Yangzhi Ma","Jie Li","Dong Liu","Li Li"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:05:57Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:05:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16162v1","title":"GOOSELoc: A Multimodal Benchmark for Ground-to-Aerial Localization in Unstructured Off-Road Terrain","abstract":"Cross-view pose estimation estimates a ground camera's geographic pose by matching onboard sensor data with geo-referenced overhead imagery. This is particularly relevant when GNSS is unreliable, for example under forest canopy or in urban canyons. Existing metric cross-view benchmarks, such as VIGOR and cross-view KITTI, are dominated by urban and road scenes with strong human-made structure without off-road terrain. We introduce GOOSELoc, a multimodal benchmark for ground-to-aerial localization in unstructured environments, including forests, fields, and rural tracks. GOOSELoc comprises 9,356 scenes recorded across all four seasons around Munich, Germany, pairing geo-referenced aerial orthophotos with surround-view RGB imagery and 3D LiDAR point clouds. Paved/unpaved labels enable analyzing the impact of terrain structure, while the seasonal recordings allow studying the appearance gap to mostly summer-captured orthophotos. To assess the transferability of existing methods, we adapt and fine-tune four state-of-the-art cross-view localization baselines on GOOSELoc. Despite task-specific fine-tuning, the performance degrades substantially in off-road environments. We therefore propose a multimodal baseline combining surround-view imagery and LiDAR geometry, improving localization performance and providing a stronger reference for future work. Data and baselines are released at https://gooseloc.github.io.","authors":["Fabian Deuser","Peter Mortimer","Robin-Nico Kampa","Anna Bößendörfer","Mirko Mählisch","Martin Werner","Norbert Oswald"],"published":"2026-11-12T01:49:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T01:49:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16162v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16162v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16162v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"}],"favorites":[],"latest":{"generated_at_utc":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","category":"cs.CV","keywords":["video","retrieval","3d","agent","representation","instance","multimodal","diffusion"],"fetch_window_hours":72,"daily_window_hours":30,"match_count":57,"new_or_recent_papers":[{"id":"2611.17336v1","title":"Back to Pixels: Lifting Latent Video Diffusion into Pixel Space","abstract":"Frontier video generation models work in a VAE latent space that compresses video in both space and time. This compression made training tractable, but now limits high-resolution, controllable, and interactive generation -- doubling resolution quadruples token count, conditions apply to groups of frames rather than individual ones, and each response must wait for the rest of its multi-frame block. We present PixVid, a simple yet effective method that lifts a pretrained latent video model to directly generate every frame in pixel space. PixVid keeps the diffusion transformer and replaces the VAE decoder with a convolutional head that renders its outputs into pixels. We find that predicting clean video rather than velocity, together with a larger noise shift, is key to quality. Finetuned from Wan2.1-14B, PixVid is competitive with the latent baseline within $\\sim 1.3$M training clips. Working framewise in pixels, it generates at $2\\times$ higher resolution with the same per-frame token count, keeps depth-conditioned video editing aligned under fast motion, and responds to each action at the next frame. Project page: https://chenshuo20.github.io/PixVid/","authors":["Yanke Song","Shuo Chen","Chris Rockwell","Guande He","Nan Liu","Desai Xie","Tianpei Gu","Yong-Hyun Park","Aryan V S","Dennis Yuren Menn","Ming-Hsuan Yang","Xun Huang"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:55Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17336v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17336v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17336v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96818,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Frontier video generation models work in a VAE latent space that compresses video in both space and time. This compression made training tractable, but now limits high-resolution, controllable, and interactive generation -- doubling resolution quadruples toke...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17334v1","title":"Unbundling Multimodal Vision Encoding","abstract":"How multimodal language models should encode visual input remains up for debate. Most models use a pretrained vision encoder, while some recent recipes train the encoder from scratch with the language model or remove it entirely. Vision encoders bundle together three design choices: a visual prior from upstream pretraining, dedicated vision capacity, and pre-fusion placement of that capacity (i.e., before the multimodal backbone). Because published models also differ in data, architecture, and compute, comparing them cannot isolate the effect of each choice. We disentangle these choices via a controlled framework where we pretrain all models from scratch on common data and compare them on visual question answering and language benchmarks at matched lifetime compute. We find that the visual prior is not necessary; an encoder trained jointly from scratch is on par with a pretrained one, without a separate training stage. What matters instead is dedicated vision capacity: an encoder-free model trails on visual understanding even when its backbone is enlarged by the encoder's parameter count. Yet that capacity need not precede multimodal fusion, as vision-only modules added in-fusion to early backbone layers perform comparably to a pre-fusion encoder and can lower time to first token. These results also hold across backbone sizes, when encoder pretraining is treated as free, and when starting from a pretrained language model. Overall, our findings favor dedicated vision capacity trained jointly with the backbone and placed early within it.","authors":["Ellis Brown","Chris Hoang","Shengyi Qian","Shang-Wen Li","Mike Lewis","Marjan Ghazvininejad","Luke Zettlemoyer","Lucas Beyer","Mengye Ren","Jakob Verbeek","Saining Xie","Hu Xu"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17334v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17334v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17334v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":206882,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：How multimodal language models should encode visual input remains up for debate. Most models use a pretrained vision encoder, while some recent recipes train the encoder from scratch with the language model or remove it entirely. Vision encoders bundle togeth...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17331v1","title":"TTB: Test-time Baking for Efficient Rendering of Decoder-only View Synthesis Models","abstract":"Decoder-only Large View Synthesis Models (LVSMs) with KV-cache have recently achieved state-of-the-art quality by regressing novel views from neural networks without reconstructing 3D geometry, while Test-Time Training (TTT) layers save computation but sacrifice quality. We propose Test-Time Baking (TTB), a strategy that learns to bake global scene information from cross-view attention into a lightweight MLP, enabling fast and high-fidelity novel-view decoding. The first key is to repurpose the fast-weight MLP of TTT to learn the cross-view attention mapping explicitly from its input-output pairs, rather than replacing attention entirely, achieving the same rendering efficiency as TTT while delivering substantially higher quality. The second key addresses a modality misalignment between context prefilling and novel-view rendering: we disentangle context tokens into camera-only context rendering tokens to serve as TTB input, and context ground-truth tokens with image content to serve as the TTB mapping target, ensuring the baked MLPs are relieved of the modality generalization challenge between context and novel-view processing. Our method achieves up to **23x** rendering speedup with quality on par with the state-of-the-art across several benchmarks. Project page: https://test-time-baking.github.io","authors":["Chin-Yang Lin","Ryo Hachiuma","Jaesung Choe","Min-Hung Chen","Yu-Chiang Frank Wang","Yen-Yu Lin","Wei-Chen Chiu","Yu-Lun Liu","Cheng Sun"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:52Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17331v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17331v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17331v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":104545,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Decoder-only Large View Synthesis Models (LVSMs) with KV-cache have recently achieved state-of-the-art quality by regressing novel views from neural networks without reconstructing 3D geometry, while Test-Time Training (TTT) layers save computation but sacrif...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17330v1","title":"ProtoVis-Nav: Prototypical Visual Imagination for Visual-Spatial Aligned UAV Navigation","abstract":"Vision language navigation (VLN) is very challenging for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) since small errors in 3D motion prediction can lead to navigation errors and collisions. Unable to fathom execution outcomes in 3D environments, existing methods often struggle to predict accurate navigation waypoints from language instructions. To solve this, we introduce the Prototypical Visual Imagination framework, shortened as ProtoVis-Nav, which forces the model to imagine an outcome-associated local visual representation for the intended next UAV movement. Specifically, we predict feature prototypes and use their linear combination to estimate the visual feature of the target region. In this manner, the VLN model first imagines the local visual representation of the next intended location, and then uses predicted prototypes as context for generating the final action, promoting alignment between visual context and waypoint outputs. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of the ProtoVis-Nav framework and show substantial improvements over existing baselines.","authors":["Guangyuan Sui","Yunzhong Hou","Chi Harold Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17330v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17330v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17330v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":68643,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision language navigation (VLN) is very challenging for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) since small errors in 3D motion prediction can lead to navigation errors and collisions. Unable to fathom execution outcomes in 3D environments, existing methods often st...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17324v1","title":"K2V: Generative Keyframing For Physically Plausible Video Generation","abstract":"Video diffusion models are able to generate samples with high-quality appearance and textural qualities. However, content produced by these methods is known to exhibit significant physical errors. This is because, in a classical video generation setting, all physical reasoning must take place simultaneously and exclusively within the diffusion process.We propose to resolve these issues by redistributing the task of physical reasoning onto a language model-backed keyframe generation module, which allows for explicit reasoning in language, and fine-grained control over the appearance of the video at important moments in the video. We then condition a video generation model with these keyframes, reducing the role of the diffusion model to the simpler task of interpolating between physically-correct intermediate states, rather than inferring an entire sequence with diffusion. Results demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms existing approaches, achieving state-of-the-art performance on the Physics-IQ Verified benchmark and PhyGenBench.","authors":["Felix O'Mahony","Daniel Olmeda Reino","Roberto Cipolla","Ayush Tewari"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:59:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:59:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17324v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17324v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17324v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":69772,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video diffusion models are able to generate samples with high-quality appearance and textural qualities. However, content produced by these methods is known to exhibit significant physical errors. This is because, in a classical video generation setting, all...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17321v1","title":"BrickFlow: Connectivity-Guided Brick Reconstruction","abstract":"Given a set of LEGO parts and an image of an assembled object, our task is to infer the pose of each part to assemble the object. In contrast with traditional 3D reconstruction, doing so requires satisfying discrete physical constraints, which provide structure but render the problem combinatorial and highly sensitive to small pose errors. To learn a prior over how parts fit together, we train a set-conditioned SE(3) flow model on procedurally generated assemblies. We then finetune this model to inject image conditioning, using 1% as much data. However, while the learned flow captures global structure, it does not enforce connectivity. To address this, we introduce an analytic connectivity-field guidance to pull predictions toward connected assemblies at test time. Through this, we demonstrate improved build quality with model size, pretraining, and guidance. We make our models and data available for research purposes at https://kulits.github.io/BrickFlow/","authors":["Peter Kulits","Cordelia Schmid"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:58:51Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:58:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17321v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17321v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17321v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":54671,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Given a set of LEGO parts and an image of an assembled object, our task is to infer the pose of each part to assemble the object. In contrast with traditional 3D reconstruction, doing so requires satisfying discrete physical constraints, which provide structu...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17318v1","title":"Spatial Foundry: A Multimodal Dataset for Spatial Intelligence","abstract":"Spatial intelligence requires connecting visual observations to the geometry, objects, and persistent state of an environment. Images alone leave these relationships implicit, while disconnected annotations make it difficult to study appearance, camera motion, and scene changes under a common reference. We introduce Spatial Foundry, a collection of 5,280 furnished residential 3D scene configurations with editable assets and aligned visual, geometric, and temporal data. Its modalities include floor plans, perspective and panoramic RGB, metric depth, point clouds, meshes, instance-level 3D bounding boxes, and camera trajectories paired with video. Shared scene coordinates link observations across views and time; editable source scenes enable additional camera paths and controlled object changes. We describe the data representation and construction workflow, and discuss applications in floor-plan-conditioned 3D generation, geometric reconstruction, instance-level perception, camera-conditioned video generation, shared world modeling, and scene editing. Controlled camera and scene variations support spatial perception and world-model research. The public project page provides videos and an interactive colored point-cloud preview.","authors":["Ethan Lin","Yang Song"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:58:36Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:58:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17318v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17318v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17318v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":42661,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, representation, instance, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Spatial intelligence requires connecting visual observations to the geometry, objects, and persistent state of an environment. Images alone leave these relationships implicit, while disconnected annotations make it difficult to study appearance, camera motion...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17298v1","title":"Consistent Video-to-Video Translation via Explicit Correspondences","abstract":"Interactive video-to-video applications require real-time generation while maintaining long-range temporal consistency. However, recent methods achieve speed by restricting attention to a fixed-length causal window. This limits long-range consistency whenever relevant history falls outside the context window. To address this limitation, we introduce vid2vid-long, a correspondence-based approach grounded in a simple observation. Video-to-video translation provides an explicit cross-frame reference: the regions that match in the input video should match in the output video too. Concretely, we compute patch-level correspondences in the input, guiding retrieval of previously generated outputs in the corresponding regions, and condition the model on these patches through correspondence cross-attention. Our experiments demonstrate that vid2vid-long consistently improves long-range temporal consistency across recent video models while preserving generation quality and real-time performance.","authors":["Gaurav Parmar","Zhengqi Li","Richard Zhang","Jun-Yan Zhu","Srinivasa Narasimhan","Eli Shechtman","Yotam Nitzan"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:55:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:55:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17298v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17298v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17298v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":77927,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, retrieval 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Interactive video-to-video applications require real-time generation while maintaining long-range temporal consistency. However, recent methods achieve speed by restricting attention to a fixed-length causal window. This limits long-range consistency whenever...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17240v1","title":"Objects as Addresses: Instance Binding in Vision Transformer Attention","abstract":"Humans naturally parse visual scenes into coherent individual objects rather than scattered features, a capability cognitive science calls object binding. While recent work shows that pretrained Vision Transformers (ViTs) intrinsically acquire this ability, how native self-attention computations mechanistically keep object instances separate remains unresolved. By studying this across eight pretrained ViTs, we found that instance separation is expressed more strongly in attention routing than in token content: selected query-key relations distinguish individual objects more reliably than value similarity or residual representations. Inside heads, patches belonging to one individual tend to read from a common image region, which we term their attention address. Visually distinct parts of one person share an address, whereas identical duplicated instances receive separate, movable addresses. These shared destinations arise in part because query interactions with the spatial structure of keys direct attention toward consistent regions for one object and elsewhere for another. In DINOv2 and most other backbones we tested, editing this query component at the selected head redirects its attention address toward another individual. Grouping patches by this query component separates same-category instances, reaching .615 FG-ARI versus .289 for the same clustering of final-layer features, which tend to merge them.","authors":["Khalil Sabri","Guillaume-Alexandre Bilodeau","Nicolas Saunier","Wassim Bouachir"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:27:11Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:27:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17240v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17240v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17240v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":86338,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Humans naturally parse visual scenes into coherent individual objects rather than scattered features, a capability cognitive science calls object binding. While recent work shows that pretrained Vision Transformers (ViTs) intrinsically acquire this ability, h...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17199v1","title":"MALNISI-Infused Graph Construction and Weighted Contrastive Loss based GAT for Unsupervised Representation Learning in HSI","abstract":"Hyperspectral images (HSI) possess discriminating spectral characteristics with respect to land utilization due to high-dimensional information. Recently, unsupervised representation learning using graph neural networks has unveiled these utilization-specific complex spectral relations. This article has three main contributions. First, it proposes an index, the Mean Absolute Local Neighborhood Inverse Spectral Index (MALNISI), which measures the similarity between two pixels based on their neighborhood information. The index is used to construct a weighted adjacency matrix, giving the HSI a graph structure. Second, it proposes an upgraded Graph Attention Network that learns compact latent representations of hyperspectral data from the constructed graph. In this network, an enhanced multi-head attention mechanism incorporates the MALNISI weights as an auxiliary feature to emphasize learned attention between neighboring pixels. To alleviate over-smoothing and preserve fine-grained spectral details, the architecture integrates residual connections, skip projections, and feature normalization, which together enhance representation capability, stabilize learning, and accelerate convergence. Third, it proposes a weighted contrastive loss function that incorporates neighborhood-aware MALNISI between connected pixels, encouraging strongly connected pixels in the high-dimensional space to remain close in the latent space, and weakly connected pixels to stay apart. The resulting discriminative low-dimensional embeddings are then clustered using spectral clustering. Experimental analysis on six benchmark HSI datasets demonstrates the efficiency and necessity of the three aforementioned contributions, and the comparative study highlights superior performance over fourteen state-of-the-art approaches.","authors":["Soumi Sarkar","Subhadip Boral","Sarbani Palit","Ashish Ghosh"],"published":"2026-11-12T18:02:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T18:02:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17199v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17199v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17199v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":74591,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Hyperspectral images (HSI) possess discriminating spectral characteristics with respect to land utilization due to high-dimensional information. Recently, unsupervised representation learning using graph neural networks has unveiled these utilization-specific...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:41+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17194v1","title":"eDMD: Endpoint Distribution Matching for Few-Step Diffusion Distillation","abstract":"Distribution matching distillation (DMD) has emerged as a leading paradigm for distilling diffusion models into few-step students, where an auxiliary fake score model tracks the student's distribution and the discrepancy between the teacher and fake predictions drives the student toward the teacher. In multi-step distillation, although only the student's final output is delivered, existing methods train the fake model on intermediate states or across all denoising steps. Consequently, the student's update signal becomes entangled with intermediate distributions, which can distort the matching direction and remain nonzero even after the student's final outputs already match the teacher distribution. To resolve this mismatch, we propose Endpoint Distribution Matching Distillation (eDMD), which trains a single shared fake model exclusively on the student's final outputs and uses it to correct every denoising step. Our theoretical analysis proves that this endpoint reference guarantees that the distribution matching term vanishes if and only if the student's final output distribution matches the teacher's, regardless of intermediate distributions. Beyond the fake reference, we further strengthen teacher supervision through a precise teacher (PT) that replaces single-step guided predictions with short multi-step trajectories on the final noise interval, paired with a calibrated query density for intermediate steps. Experiments on SD3-Medium, FLUX.2-klein (4B and 9B), and Qwen-Image-2.1 demonstrate that eDMD consistently outperforms existing distribution matching methods for 4-step generation.","authors":["Tong Zhao","Xianfang Zeng","Yucheng Han","Ruoyu Wang","Mingkun Lei","Gang Yu","Chi Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:57:48Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:57:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17194v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17194v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17194v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":137944,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Distribution matching distillation (DMD) has emerged as a leading paradigm for distilling diffusion models into few-step students, where an auxiliary fake score model tracks the student's distribution and the discrepancy between the teacher and fake predictio...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17190v1","title":"Consensus Capture in Reference-Based UAV Video Stabilization: A Benchmark on Dynamic Urban Scenes","abstract":"Reference-frame stabilization registers every frame of a hovering-drone video onto one reference frame, keeping object positions comparable. The registration pipelines it relies on are selected on feature-matching benchmarks in which only the camera moves. Over a busy intersection, vehicles moving together supply mutually consistent correspondences that can draw the robust homography estimate toward their motion: consensus capture, which inlier statistics cannot flag. A two-part benchmark measures it and the object masking meant to prevent it. A static protocol scores ten pipelines under exactly known homographies on 4K aerial frames, tuned on a site-disjoint split at a matched resolution and feature budget. A dynamic protocol scores them at an offline and a real-time budget on hovers selected from the video alone to span the amount and coherence of moving foreground, plus a stationary control. It measures background alignment, object position error and the share of a moving group's displacement an estimate follows, against a pseudo-ground-truth reference audited with manually annotated ground control points, which also score every candidate. On static scenes, tuned AKAZE, RootSIFT, SIFT and BRISK lead, retuning the matcher thresholds raises the mean average accuracy (mAA) by at most 0.0062, and masking costs less than 0.005. Unmasked ORB is captured on 28 of 304 real-time dynamic pairs and on 6 offline; its real-time captured estimates have a median corner error of 18.8 px yet typical inlier counts and ratios. Masking removes capture for every pipeline and leaves the leading pipelines unchanged. Code, data and results are released.","authors":["Robert Fonod","Nikolas Geroliminis"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:55:06Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:55:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17190v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17190v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17190v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":268210,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Reference-frame stabilization registers every frame of a hovering-drone video onto one reference frame, keeping object positions comparable. The registration pipelines it relies on are selected on feature-matching benchmarks in which only the camera moves. Ov...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17146v1","title":"HiVE-VQA: Hierarchical Interaction via Volumetric Embedding for Video Quality Assessment","abstract":"The proliferation of user-generated video content has made accurate and robust no-reference video quality assessment (NR-VQA) a highly important challenge. Existing research increasingly relies on architecturally complex multi-branch pipelines or computationally intensive fine-tuning of single-branch backbones. In this paper, we investigate whether a single frozen video foundation model can serve as an effective NR-VQA backbone, and propose HiVE-VQA, the first framework (to our knowledge) to apply a JEPA-based video encoder to this task. HiVE-VQA extracts token grids from multiple depths of a pre-trained V-JEPA 2.1 encoder and stacks them into a hierarchical spatio-temporal volume, which a compact 3D convolutional head fuses across its layer, spatial, and temporal axes, combining low-level distortion cues from shallow layers with the semantic and temporal context captured at greater depths. The encoder remains frozen throughout, so only the head is trained: 5.2M parameters (5.7\\% of the total), with no auxiliary multi-modal models and an inference cost that is invariant to video resolution. Trained solely on LSVQ and evaluated without any fine-tuning, HiVE-VQA outperforms 17 state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods in SROCC and PLCC on both intra-dataset (LSVQ-test, LSVQ-1080p) and cross-dataset (KoNViD-1k, LIVE-VQC) benchmarks. These results indicate that hierarchical, depth-wise fusion of features from a single frozen video foundation model provides a more effective and efficient basis for NR-VQA than task-specific multi-network fusion. Code and model checkpoints are available at https://github.com/RobHam99/HiVE-VQA.","authors":["Robbie Hamilton","Fan Zhang","David Bull"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:32:10Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:32:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17146v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17146v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17146v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":53917,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：The proliferation of user-generated video content has made accurate and robust no-reference video quality assessment (NR-VQA) a highly important challenge. Existing research increasingly relies on architecturally complex multi-branch pipelines or computationa...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17111v1","title":"Spatiotemporal learning improves quantification of pore-space evolution and fluid displacement","abstract":"Pore-space evolution and fluid displacement influence how rocks transmit and store fluids, affecting geological carbon storage, underground hydrogen storage and groundwater contaminant transport. A single time-resolved micro-CT experiment may produce tens to hundreds of three-dimensional images, and segmentation is required to quantify physical properties and run image-based simulations. Traditional methods such as thresholding perform poorly on noisy, low-contrast images and need manual adjustment per image; 3D deep learning networks segment each image without temporal information, so their errors vary between images and could be mistaken for physical change. Here we introduce a transformer-based 4D segmentation framework combining spatial and temporal information. It uses sequential images to resolve ambiguous phase assignments and separate physical change from segmentation error. The method is tested on images of mineral dissolution: it measures pore-volume growth and rock boundaries more accurately than an equivalent 3D model that ignores temporal information. It also captures the increase in permeability verified against reference segmentations and flow simulations. The advantage transfers to another dissolution sample without retraining. On two other datasets of fluid displacement, it reduces spurious changes in predicted rock and porosity fluctuations and accurately identifies filling events. Spatiotemporal segmentation turns sequences of hundreds of three-dimensional images into more reliable quantitative measurements of pore-space evolution and fluid displacement without per-image parameter adjustment to support the analysis of time-resolved CT imaging.","authors":["Zhuangzhuang Ma","Rukuan Chai","Qianqian Ma","Yanghua Wang","Branko Bijeljic","Martin J. Blunt"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:04:50Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:04:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17111v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17111v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17111v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":134525,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Pore-space evolution and fluid displacement influence how rocks transmit and store fluids, affecting geological carbon storage, underground hydrogen storage and groundwater contaminant transport. A single time-resolved micro-CT experiment may produce tens to...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17105v1","title":"Predicting Ethanol Drinking Status from Nonhuman Primate Liver Histology: A Proof of Concept for Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning in Few-Shot Vision-Language Modeling","abstract":"Combining vision-language models (VLMs) with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) offers a promising approach to few-shot whole-slide image (WSI) classification in computational pathology. This study evaluates this approach for predicting ethanol drinking status from nonhuman primate liver histology. Adapters are evaluated against non-PEFT configurations on a subset of the Monkey Alcohol Tissue Research Resource (MATRR). All evaluated PEFT configurations achieve higher mean balanced accuracy than the non-PEFT baselines. For example, in the four-shot setting, attention-based multiple instance learning (ABMIL) alone achieves 43.5% mean balanced accuracy, compared with 78.1% after adding an adapter. Qualitative attention analysis shows that textual pathway adaptations produce localized attention heatmaps, while visual pathway adaptations produce more diffuse ones. A pathologist's review identified similar histological features across adaptations.","authors":["Amirmohammad Mohammadi","Rupak Khadka","Kathleen A. Grant","Mary Lauren Benton","Joshua Peeples"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:02:27Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:02:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17105v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17105v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17105v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":19062,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Combining vision-language models (VLMs) with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) offers a promising approach to few-shot whole-slide image (WSI) classification in computational pathology. This study evaluates this approach for predicting ethanol drinking s...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17104v1","title":"TuMAP: A Dual-View, Morphotype-Aware Pathomics Pipeline for Quantifying Renal Tubular Remodeling","abstract":"Quantifying disease-associated renal tubular remodeling is challenging because tubular appearances are heterogeneous. We introduce TuMAP (Tubular Morphotype-Aware Pathomics), a pipeline combining (1) dual-view foundation-model representations and provisional morphotype annotation, (2) compositional and within-morphotype pathomics, and (3) patient-level statistical inference. From 456,158 tubules in 358 PAS-stained kidney biopsy slides, TuMAP identified eight image-based morphotypes, seven of which showed stable subsampling assignments. Across 219 patients in six diagnostic groups, diabetic nephropathy (DN) and tubulointerstitial nephritis/acute tubular injury (TIN/ATI) each differed from minimal change disease in seven morphotype proportions, while IgA nephropathy differed in six. DN showed reduced nuclear solidity and increased eccentricity across all eight morphotypes. Area-normalized nuclear density exposed additional within-morphotype differences. TuMAP provides a framework for separating changes in tubular morphotype composition from phenotypic differences within morphotypes.","authors":["Ruijie Wu","Yuechen Yang","Yanfan Zhu","Yu Wang","Shilin Zhao","Ming-Tsun Tsai","Szu-Yuan Li","Shuo-Ming Ou","Tz-Heng Chen","Haichun Yang","Yuankai Huo"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:02:18Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:02:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17104v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17104v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17104v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":20856,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Quantifying disease-associated renal tubular remodeling is challenging because tubular appearances are heterogeneous. We introduce TuMAP (Tubular Morphotype-Aware Pathomics), a pipeline combining (1) dual-view foundation-model representations and provisional...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17103v1","title":"Joint Null-Space Projection for Data-Free Model Merging","abstract":"Merging independently fine-tuned checkpoints into a single multi-task model requires no data, no labels, and no gradient steps. In practice the task vectors of different tasks interfere, and the standard remedies--averaging, magnitude pruning, sign election--operate entry by entry, ignoring that each delta induces a low-dimensional update on the hidden features it acts upon. We recast merging as a subspace interference problem. For every two-dimensional weight tensor we take the top-r right-singular subspace of each task's delta--the input directions along which that task moves the representation most--and remove from each task vector the component lying in the union of the other tasks' subspaces. The resulting Joint Null-Space Projection (JNSP) is closed-form and order-independent, needs no forward pass, and is defined entirely by the checkpoint set. On eight CLIP image-classification tasks JNSP merges ViT-B/16 experts to 89.45% average accuracy without using a single image, 11.88 points above the strongest parameter-wise training-free baseline (TIES-Merging, 77.57%), 14.22 above Task Arithmetic, and 0.98 above WUDI's data-free consensus merge (88.47%). The gain holds across three backbones on the independently trained TALL release (+10.8, +12.6, +9.1 over TIES for ViT-B/16, ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14) and replicates there at 89.20%; controls confirm the source--a single shared subspace instead of per-task ones loses 2.9 points, and a sequential variant collapses to 69.6%. On RoBERTa-large the same projection underperforms plain task arithmetic, which we report rather than hide. Code is available at https://github.com.","authors":["Tian Xia"],"published":"2026-11-12T17:01:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T17:01:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17103v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17103v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17103v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":176818,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Merging independently fine-tuned checkpoints into a single multi-task model requires no data, no labels, and no gradient steps. In practice the task vectors of different tasks interfere, and the standard remedies--averaging, magnitude pruning, sign election--...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17060v1","title":"Ropedia Xperience 2: A Gold Standard for Scaling Physical AI Environments and Behaviors","abstract":"Large foundation models such as GPT-6 Astra and Claude Fable 5.1 have recently been shown to control robots directly from visual observations. They nevertheless remain brittle on long-horizon, contact-rich tasks in real-world environments. Further progress depends on two complementary resources: demonstrations of human behavior that supervise how tasks are performed, and interactive environments in which policies can be rolled out, evaluated, and improved in closed loop. Language models have advanced along well-characterized scaling laws; the corresponding question for physical AI is how capability scales with environments and behaviors, which first requires identifying which data to scale and how to produce and reuse such data continuously. To address this question, we introduce Ropedia Xperience 2, comprising 1,589 released metric-scale 3D scans and a collection of 4,230 egocentric recordings of 74 tasks (131 hours). We further show that each capture can be repurposed for robot learning: scenes are converted into physically interactive simulation environments, human motion is retargeted to whole-body robot trajectories, and additional robot behaviors are obtained through virtual teleoperation and automated long-horizon trajectory generation. We propose this joint capture of environments and behaviors as a gold standard for physical AI data collection, laying a data foundation for establishing scaling laws over environments and behaviors.","authors":["Hao Li","Fangzhou Hong","Zhaoxi Chen","Hui En Pang","Kairui Hu","Mingze Sun","Runmao Yao","Xinhao Ji","Xiyuan Zheng","Xuanli Chen","Yalun Dai","Yap Kim Hui","Dingwen Zhang","Yuanyuan Gao","Ziang Cao","Cheng Huang Yean","Jackson Peng","Rui Duan","Yaobo Liu","Zetong Wang","Ziwei Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T16:37:21Z","updated":"2026-11-12T16:37:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17060v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17060v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17060v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":78554,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Large foundation models such as GPT-6 Astra and Claude Fable 5.1 have recently been shown to control robots directly from visual observations. They nevertheless remain brittle on long-horizon, contact-rich tasks in real-world environments. Further progress de...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.17048v1","title":"AURA 2.0: From User-Driven Responses to Model-Initiated Conversations in Streaming Video","abstract":"Most AI assistants respond only when prompted, remaining silent even when events in the video stream offer meaningful opportunities for conversation. To move beyond purely user-driven interaction, we present AURA 2.0, a streaming video large language model that introduces three core upgrades to AURA. The key advance is Model-Initiated Conversation (MIC), which enables the model to identify topics worth discussing in the evolving visual stream and initiate conversations at appropriate moments without a user query. Stream-Aware Tool Calling (SATC) enables the model to invoke external tools using the current visual and conversational context and incorporate returned information into the ongoing interaction. Interactive Visual Grounding (IVG) supports visual grounding in both user inputs and model outputs. The model can identify objects marked by users on video frames and produce frame-aligned bounding boxes to link its responses to specific objects and regions. To develop these capabilities, we construct a dedicated streaming interaction dataset and adopt a three-stage training scheme to integrate the new behaviors with AURA's existing streaming interaction abilities. We further incorporate Long-Horizon Interaction Memory (LHIM) to maintain contextual continuity over extended video streams. Evaluations on public streaming benchmarks show that AURA 2.0 achieves leading performance among open-source streaming models, while targeted evaluations further validate its new interaction capabilities. We open-source the model weights and inference code.","authors":["Shuhan Li","Huankang Guan","Xintong Guo","Yang Bo","Yuyang He","Dunyuan Xu","Jingyan Chen","Fang Liu","Tsz Kin Wan","Jinpeng Chen","Zhenqi He","Yuxuan Hu","Wenbo Li","Han Gao","Qijun Li","Long Chen","Hongsheng Li","Rui Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T16:31:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T16:31:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17048v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.17048v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17048v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":81044,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Most AI assistants respond only when prompted, remaining silent even when events in the video stream offer meaningful opportunities for conversation. To move beyond purely user-driven interaction, we present AURA 2.0, a streaming video large language model th...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16925v1","title":"Report of the 8th LSVOS Challenge: Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding","abstract":"This report summarizes the Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding (MUMU) track of the 8th Large-scale Video Object Segmentation (LSVOS) Challenge, held in conjunction with ECCV 2026. MUMU asks for a single unified model that jointly performs multi-concept image tagging over quality, scene, and event vocabularies, open-vocabulary object detection, and concise image captioning, within a budget of 0.5B parameters and 8 GB of peak inference memory. Submissions are ranked by a weighted task score scaled by an efficiency factor. We describe the tasks, rules, and evaluation protocol, report the final results, and review the methods of the top three teams. All three leading solutions build on the compact Florence-2-base model, but extend it in different ways: lightweight tagging heads with cross-task inference refinement, a shared low-rank adapter trained on coverage-aware multi-source data with task-dependent decoding, and a training-free pipeline that maps captions to tags and applies detection test-time augmentation. The results show that a compact prompt-based vision-language model can serve all three outputs when supervision, decoding, and cross-task cues are designed for each task, while open-vocabulary detection on novel categories remains the main bottleneck.","authors":["Chang Liu","Henghui Ding","Tong Yang","Dayoung Kil","Seong-heum Kim","Qilin Xiang","Ronghao Zheng","Ziyang Zhang","Shanlin Jiang","Ruohan Shi","Qilin Fan","Chengfeng Qiu","Kaifeng Wei"],"published":"2026-11-12T15:14:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T15:14:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16925v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16925v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16925v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":36081,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：This report summarizes the Mobile Unified Multimodal Understanding (MUMU) track of the 8th Large-scale Video Object Segmentation (LSVOS) Challenge, held in conjunction with ECCV 2026. MUMU asks for a single unified model that jointly performs multi-concept im...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:42+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16897v1","title":"TokenStream: Multi-Subject Personalized Video Generation with Token-wise LoRA and Guided Attention","abstract":"We introduce TokenStream, an optimization-based framework that composes independently learned subject identities in text-to-video generation without a reference-video training dataset or joint optimization of each new subject combination. Learning from a single reference image makes subject appearance difficult to separate from static-image bias, while merging independently trained adapters can mix identities. Token-wise LoRA (ToRA) restricts low-rank updates to the cross-attention value projections of subject-associated text tokens, providing a separate adaptation path for each subject. A T2V-guided stream copies queries and keys from the frozen base model during early denoising to reuse its layout and motion priors while retaining personalized values. We also introduce Multi-DB, a 30-subject, 122-prompt benchmark with appearance-free prompts and varied interactions. On Multi-DB at 1.3B, TokenStream improves identity preservation from 0.422 for DreamBooth to 0.467 and textual alignment from 0.652 to 0.688.","authors":["Hung Nguyen","Quang Qui-Vinh Nguyen","Khoi Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:54:14Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:54:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16897v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16897v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16897v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16869v1","title":"Raysemble: Metric 3D Bone Reconstruction from a Single X-ray","abstract":"Raysemble reconstructs eight lumbar and pelvic bones from a single X-ray by separating shape and placement. Each 3D query samples regional and global features at its image projection. A shared decoder predicts normalized bone shapes. Predicted depth and camera geometry determine 3D centers and scales for resampling these shapes onto a common grid in millimeters. Evaluation uses 23 held-out patients with 43 CT scans, each generating one simulated anteroposterior radiograph. Without rigid alignment to CT,Raysemble leads 15 evaluated methods, averaging 65.24% Dice, 9.09 mm HD95, and 3.32 mm ASSD over three random seeds. With identical inputs, it improves Dice over VolumeNeRF by 5.86 percentage points.","authors":["Xiaoyang Li","Xinghao Yan","Yuan Chai"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:38:06Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:38:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16869v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16869v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16869v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16861v1","title":"Visual Cipher Bench: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Long-Horizon Visual Reasoning","abstract":"While language-model agents excel at long-horizon coding and terminal tasks, this success relies on two conditions: explicit text specifications and immediate execution feedback. However, neither condition holds in real-world problems where critical information exists only in images, leaving it unclear whether long-horizon capability transfers from text to visual reasoning. In such visual settings, because rules and clues resist text transcription and lack test-case feedback, agents must repeatedly inspect images, simulate spatial transformations, and autonomously cross-check clues. Despite this challenge, existing evaluations fail to capture such capability: visual reasoning benchmarks largely reduce tasks to single-turn, short-chain question answering, whereas agent benchmarks focus on text-based environments or interactive states that permit trial-and-error. To bridge this gap, we introduce Visual Cipher Bench, a collection of 40 original visual puzzles across four categories: spatial manipulation and simulation, logical constraints and planning, rule induction, and knowledge-based decoding. Each puzzle provides only one to three black-and-white line drawings and a minimal submission instruction, requiring agents to discover the underlying solving mechanism entirely from the images. Across 29 reasoning-effort configurations of eight frontier models, even the strongest model, GPT-6 Astra (max), achieves only 26.7% accuracy, while five of the eight models score below 1%. Yet on a ten-task subset, providing a single non-spoiler mechanism hint boosts accuracy from 7.8% to 55.6%. This sharp contrast shows that the primary bottleneck of current agents lies not in executing multi-step plans, but in autonomously discovering rules from visual evidence. Visual Cipher Bench thus provides a rigorous benchmark for measuring long-horizon visual reasoning in agents.","authors":["Deyao Hong","Jiayi Jiang","Yuchun Chen","Yixuan Shi","Wenyi Li","Zheng Jiang","Chengxing Xie","Mingyu Su","Qingle Liu","Mingju Gao","Yizhe Chi","Youjie Zheng","Calvin Xiao","Kaisen Yang","Huan-ang Gao"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:33:46Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:33:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16861v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16861v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16861v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16840v1","title":"Kosmo-GFM: A Production-Ready Geometry Foundation Model","abstract":"We present Kosmo-GFM, a scene reconstruction system that integrates a geometry foundation model (GFM), a pose optimization engine (PoseEngine), and reconstruction-derived supervision from KosmoWorld. GFM predicts camera parameters, depth, cross-view correspondences, and dynamic 3D points from images and videos. PoseEngine combines these predictions with geometric optimization to recover scene-level camera poses and geometry for downstream 3D and 4D Gaussian Splatting. KosmoWorld generates images and geometric supervision from reconstructed real-world scenes to improve GFM's geometric predictions. On the Sparse and Medium subsets of SpatialBench, GFM achieves the lowest absolute relative depth error and the highest relative-pose AUC at 5 degrees among the compared methods. It also achieves the lowest mean endpoint errors for dynamic points and scene flow across the five evaluated datasets. Across 34 scenes completed by all compared reconstruction systems, PoseEngine achieves the highest scene-averaged pose AUC at thresholds of 1, 3, and 5 degrees, with a cumulative runtime of 14.07 hours, approximately 20% of GLUEMAP's 69.67 hours. PoseEngine Flash provides a further accuracy-efficiency trade-off, with a measured mixed-precision runtime of 2.62 hours, approximately 4% of GLUEMAP's runtime. Separate scaling experiments show generally favorable trends with increased data, model computation, and training budgets. These results support the use of learned geometric priors within a scene-level reconstruction pipeline.","authors":["Sheng Tang","Shengzhe Wei","Tiancheng Chen","Wenhua Jin","Xinyang Lu","Tinghao Jiang","Shuoxuan Feng","Yupeng Liu","Yu Zhang","Xingshuo Chang","Keren Zhou","Yuhan Xiong","Jiahao Li","Haoming Liu","Jituo Tian","Hui Liang","Chaoqun Tang","Feng Tian","Wentao Ouyang","Kaojin Zhu","Xiaolin Chai","Peihuang Fu","Miao Zheng","Yunbo Hu","Feixue Cai","Zizhen Zou","Weigang Wang","Yexuan Yang","Haoyu Feng","Weiqi Zhang","Juntong Fang","Zesong Li"],"published":"2026-11-12T14:17:22Z","updated":"2026-11-12T14:17:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16840v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16840v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16840v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16767v1","title":"Evaluating Video LLMs' Understanding of Corner Cases in Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Natural language explanations promised by modern Video Large Language Models (Video LLMs) offer autonomous driving systems a path toward detailed scene description and interpretable decision making on the road. While recent benchmarks have begun assessing these models on driving scenes, they mainly target routine traffic conditions and general comprehension tasks based on existing driving datasets and therefore fail to evaluate model performance on the rarest and most safety-critical driving events, also known as \"corner cases\". To provide a comprehensive evaluation of Video LLMs on precisely these scenarios, we introduce DashBench, a benchmark testing 25 Video LLMs on 1,709 carefully curated driving videos spanning not only collision-related corner cases but also rare non-collision ones. Through extensive prompts and open-ended question answering, we expose a critical capability gap: all evaluated models perform substantially below the threshold required for safety-critical driving applications, frequently misjudging or missing dangerous events on the road ahead and hallucinating non-existent agents or actions in their explanations. We believe DashBench, combining an extensive dataset with a rigorous evaluation toolkit and a detailed diagnosis of current model limitations, drives progress toward safer, more explainable autonomous driving systems. The code and data will be shared at: cornercase-dashbench.github.io","authors":["Gabriel Manalu","Minh Son Hoang","Hyeontaek Hwang","Jaesung Lee","Hyojin Choi","Tran Tuan Phong","Seon Kwon Kim","Saad Wazir","Daeyoung Kim"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:15:27Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:15:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16767v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16767v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16767v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16752v1","title":"AgriSense: Vision-Language Decision Support and Robotic Intervention in a Virtual Greenhouse Copy title","abstract":"Autonomous greenhouse management increasingly requires robotic systems that integrate perception, navigation, environmental regulation, and plant-level intervention. This paper presents AgriSense, a framework combining an Isaac Sim virtual greenhouse, a quadruped mobile manipulator, RGB and LiDAR-inertial sensing, environmental control, and supervised vision-language decision support. Frozen DINOv2 and BERT encoders, together with structured environmental inputs, support joint prediction of twelve environmental references, a target zone, and an intervention flag. Checkpoint replay reproduces 41/43 zone decisions. Across five seeds on a source-grouped test set, the full, image-removed, and language-removed configurations each achieve 43/44 correct zone decisions, providing no evidence of incremental multimodal zone-accuracy improvement. The full model achieves mean absolute errors of 0.249 degrees C for temperature, 2.030 percentage points for humidity, and 0.997 percentage points for soil moisture. Sensor-only inputs match full-model zone accuracy, while language-only inputs achieve 68.64%. External real-image benchmarks reveal distribution-shift limitations. Five offline Point-LIO routes yield rigidly aligned trajectory errors of 5.22-17.32 mm. Of 40 virtual-arm targets, 32 satisfy geometric acceptance criteria and eight are rejected at boundaries. Six long-horizon climate cases and fault protocols characterize integration limits. Navigation and integrated treatment rely on simulator-state inputs; physical greenhouse performance and agronomic treatment efficacy remain unvalidated. The contribution is an evaluated decision-support and robotic-intervention framework for a virtual greenhouse.","authors":["Lyes Saad Saoud","Oualid Doukhi","Reza Ghorbani","Moussa Ayyash"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:04:57Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:04:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16752v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16752v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16752v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16749v1","title":"ORCA:Orthogonal Residual Consensus Alignment for Multi-View Clustering","abstract":"Deep multi-view clustering often suffers from redundant information accumulation during fusion and indiscriminate alignment across representation levels. We propose Orthogonal Residual Consensus Alignment (ORCA) to address these challenges. Its semantic incremental recurrent fusion module extracts cross-view orthogonal residuals as non-redundant increments and adaptively weights recurrent states through divergence-guided gating. Its consensus-calibrated hierarchical contrastive alignment module uses the global consensus to weight contrastive alignment of view-level, pairwise, and recurrent representations. Together, these modules balance view-specific structure with cross-view consistency. Experiments on five benchmarks against fifteen competing methods demonstrate ORCA's effectiveness, including a 5.25 percent accuracy improvement over the strongest baseline on 100leaves. Ablation studies validate both modules. Code is available at https://github.com/XiaojianDing/2026-NeurIPS-ORCA.","authors":["Xiaojian Ding","Xian Li","Xiaoying Zhu","Lin Zhao","Kaixiang Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T13:00:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T13:00:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16749v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16749v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16749v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16748v1","title":"PhysLoop: Harnessing LLMs' Physics Awareness for Self-Improving Video Generation Models","abstract":"Video generation models produce visually realistic videos but still struggle with physical dynamics and causal consistency. Learning from generated videos requires fine-grained physical supervision, yet reliable quality judgments, correction pairs, and controlled variations are difficult to obtain. Large language models (LLMs) possess physical knowledge, but struggle to reliably guide video generation models in producing their training videos. We present PhysLoop, a unified harness designed to support physically grounded self-improvement in video generation models. The harness guides and constrains LLMs throughout video generation, selection, and correction, distilling their physical knowledge into physically consistent videos. The framework supports controllable training-data construction and inference-time video repair. By identifying and correcting failures, PhysLoop marks a key milestone toward recursive self-improvement (RSI) in video generation models with limited human intervention. We release 100K videos spanning diverse, realistic indoor and outdoor scenes, along with generation tools and two evaluation datasets: PhysLoop-Gen and PhysLoop-Repair. We evaluate controllable generation and localized repair on these datasets, respectively, and assess physical behavior on the external Physics-IQ Verified benchmark.","authors":["Yuxuan Chen","Xiaojie Li","Hao Chen","Juncheng Ma","Kewei Zhang","Zhongjing Du","Guoqiang Wei","Fangfang Wang","Zhen Dong","Zhiyang Dou","Liming Zhao","Yan Zeng","Jianwei Zhang","Daquan Zhou"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:59:35Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:59:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16748v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16748v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16748v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16742v1","title":"Fog-FWL: A Large-Scale Full-Waveform LiDAR Dataset for Recovering Fog-Hidden Objects","abstract":"LiDAR is a core sensor for autonomous driving, yet it is highly vulnerable to fog. Fog scattering produces strong backscatter near the sensor and attenuates reflections from objects behind it, so weak object returns are often discarded by the peak detection that converts received signals into point clouds. Full-waveform LiDAR (FWL), which records the received optical signal before peak detection, retains these weak returns, but the lack of suitable data has prevented research on recovering fog-hidden objects from FWL. We present Fog-FWL, a dataset foundation for this task that consists of a real-world benchmark, Fog-FWL-Real, and a large-scale synthetic training set, Fog-FWL-Sim. Fog-FWL-Real contains 7,527 real foggy FWL frames from 8 scenes and 58 sequences, which is 579x larger than the only existing real foggy FWL data, together with histogram-level Object/Fog/Noise labels, 10,719 3D bounding boxes, and three difficulty levels defined by the attenuation of object reflections. Fog-FWL-Sim contains 12,000 frames generated by our physics-based simulator, Fog-FWL-Simulator, which integrates spatially heterogeneous fog density fields, time-resolved light transport including multiple scattering, and a photon-counting SPAD receiver model. The two subsets share the same sensor format and annotation protocol, enabling a strict sim-to-real evaluation in which no real waveforms are used for training. Our analysis shows that, under the hardest condition, object returns are included in the three strongest peaks for only 34.9% of object rays, underscoring the need for waveform-level recovery. Fog-FWL-Real, Fog-FWL-Sim, and the Fog-FWL-Simulator will be publicly released to facilitate research on fog-robust LiDAR perception.","authors":["Kazuma Ikeda","Kotaro Oishi","Ryosei Hara","Ryo Yoshida","Takahiro Kado","Ibuki Fujioka","Taro Beppu","Mariko Isogawa","Kentaro Yoshioka"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:56:55Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:56:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16742v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16742v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16742v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16727v1","title":"From Foundation VLM to Versatile Driving VLM: A Triplet Incremental Training Approach","abstract":"Adapting general Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to Autonomous Driving (AD) is thought to require heavy 3D spatial priors or intrusive architectural overhauls to compensate for a lack of spatial grounding. In this work, we present a counterintuitive pilot study: off-the-shelf general VLMs already exhibit remarkable adaptability across diverse driving tasks, while precise metric depth remains the primary missing link, a bottleneck readily addressable via onboard sensors and depth foundation models. To this end, we introduce the Triplet Incremental Training Framework, a non-intrusive paradigm that seamlessly instills 3D geometric awareness into a general VLM backbone while fully preserving its open-world capabilities. Driven by automatically constructed (image, depth, QA pair) triplets, our framework operates via a unified tri-stream training pipeline: an unfrozen primary VLM stream to adapt to driving tasks, an auxiliary depth stream that guides the visual backbone to implicitly imagine 3D scene geometry via explicit geometric constraints, and a frozen twin VLM stream employing logit-level KL distillation to prevent semantic drift. Crucially, the auxiliary depth branch is discarded during deployment, ensuring the enhanced geometric awareness comes with zero inference overhead. We evaluate our framework across four major benchmarks targeting four diverse capabilities spanning 3D spatial perception, driving-oriented language reasoning, end-to-end planning, and general multimodal understanding. Our approach outperforms state-of-the-art driving VLMs, rivals domain-specific expert models in 3D perception, and successfully retains its original general reasoning capabilities. This demonstrates that Triplet Incremental Training offers a unified, deployment-ready paradigm for transforming general foundation models into robust driving intelligences.","authors":["Shengtao Wu","Zichao Dong","Weixuan Qiao","Xiaolu Liu","Xin Zhan","Junbo Chen","Jianke Zhu"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:39:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:39:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16701v1","title":"Sprint3D: Accelerating End-to-End 3D Asset Generation through Pipeline Optimization","abstract":"Image-to-3D generative models now produce asset-quality textured meshes, but at minutes per asset they remain slow to iterate with and costly to deploy. Prior work has mostly accelerated the sampling of the networks, and where it also optimizes the mesh processing, it trains its own generators. We instead start from a publicly released pipeline and ask how much of its end-to-end time can be saved. Profiling the public Pixal3D pipeline shows that its latency is dominated by what happens after the networks: the four rectified flows take 14% of the wall-clock time and mesh post-processing takes 85%, most of it in CPU-based UV unwrapping. We therefore first rebuild the mesh post-processing on the GPU and take it off the critical path, then cut the per-evaluation overhead of the flows and distill them from 76 to 8 network evaluations. The resulting pipeline, Sprint3D, reduces the median time per asset from 133 s to 4.05 s on one H200 GPU at the same export settings (33$\\times$). At the same time, on 2,653 held-out Toys4K objects it improves every input-view normal metric of the Pixal3D protocol, with a slightly lower mean silhouette IoU, lower point-cloud alignment scores and less fine texture detail. The rebuilt post-processing alone also speeds up TRELLIS.2, which uses the same export code, by 6.7$\\times$ without a detectable change in the evaluated metrics.","authors":["Sho Kuno","Ruihan Yu","Yonghao Yu","Guixu Lin","Lian Fu","Muyao Niu","Yu-Ju Tsai","Zheng-Hui Huang","Yi-Chuan Huang","Siqi Yang","Fengbo Lan","Erwin Wu","Kaipeng Zhang","Zhixiang Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T12:18:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T12:18:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16701v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16701v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16701v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16655v1","title":"MuViRL: Multimodal View-Invariant Visual Speech Representation Learning via Calibrated View Aggregation","abstract":"Multimodal representation learning combines complementary signals to capture shared semantic information. For dynamic scenes, these representations need to remain consistent across views while preserving distinctions between temporal states. In visual speech representation learning, view variation changes the visibility and reliability of articulation cues, making it difficult to learn such representations from synchronized audio and video. To address this challenge, we propose MuViRL, a self-supervised framework for multimodal view-invariant visual speech representation learning via calibrated view aggregation. The framework combines synchronized 3D Gaussian view synthesis with multimodal self-supervision to learn consistent representations from real and synthesized data. A multimodal decision model learns relative view utility from teacher prediction losses, and its calibrated scores guide the aggregation of complementary information. Aggregation-to-Single Distillation transfers the aggregated predictions to a single branch. Real-synthetic alignment matches synchronized windows across views while preserving their temporal relations. Experiments on real and synthesized multiview data demonstrate that MuViRL improves multimodal representation consistency and visual speech recognition performance across views.","authors":["Fei Su","Kai Qiu","Juan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:31:59Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:31:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16655v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16655v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16655v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16639v1","title":"Asymmetric Multimodal Learning with Decomposed JEPAs","abstract":"Cross-modal alignment is the standard objective for building multimodal representations. We show that alignment as a \\emph{sole} objective is lossy wherever modalities are informationally asymmetric---where neither can express what the other carries. Visual question answering is an obvious case: a question is a selective relational operator probing a dense visual field whose fine-grained features no query recovers, so what alignment preserves is not what the task requires. We prove an \\textbf{Alignment Redundancy Bound}: alignment-only Dirichlet energy minimization retains at most the redundant information atom $\\Red(Z; X_1, X_2)$, annihilating private modality-specific structure $\\Unq(Z; X_m)$ and cross-modal synergy $\\Syn(Z; X_1, X_2)$. We introduce \\textbf{Decomposed JEPA (D-JEPA)}, which factorizes latents into an aligned shared core and private modality-specific fibers. The shared core retains most of the structure alignment targets; the private fibers recover what alignment discards, via contextual masked prediction and asymmetric cross-subspace independence. Under information asymmetry, alignment-only training undergoes catastrophic semantic collapse on compositional reasoning benchmarks, surviving only on binary queries. D-JEPA averts this collapse, substantially improving downstream accuracy over both alignment-only and alignment-with-reconstruction baselines, and restoring a positive multimodal synergy gap in place of the baseline's inverted gap. Information-theoretic audits confirm that D-JEPA recovers most of the mutual information available from a frozen backbone, and causal fiber-swap interventions demonstrate that the recovered information is causally localized in the private fibers.","authors":["Yusuf Shihata"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:13:13Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:13:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16639v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16639v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16639v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16630v1","title":"When Chekhov's Gun Fires Anyway: Over-Realization in Text-to-Image Models","abstract":"In text-to-image generation, a target that the prompt mentions often appears in the generated image in a way the prompt does not call for. We call this failure over-realization. Mental-spaces theory points to the situations where it can happen: under a negation, inside what a character believes or imagines, as the vehicle of a figurative comparison, or behind a viewpoint that hides the target. Prior work has studied only negation in depth, and the conventional alignment and preference metrics do not penalize the failure. We build OverReal to measure it: OverReal-Gen, 698 prompts covering the four situations, on which we measure each generator's over-realization rate; and OverReal-Det, 2,841 generated images human-labeled for whether and how the target is over-realized, on which we verify the automatic judge that produces this rate. Our evaluation of eight text-to-image systems reveals that all of them over-realize, and that conventional metrics such as CLIPScore reward the failure rather than penalize it. Prompt expansion only partly mitigates the failure. Inside the generator, the diffusion backbone attends to a mentioned target almost as much as to a requested one. The cue words that should hold the target back receive less attention than ordinary words.","authors":["Jiahao Huang","Kon Woo Kim","Song-Duo Ma","Yuka Kitamura","Felix Wagner","Yunshan Huang","Sunisth Kumar","Xanh Ho","Yun-Ang Wu","Zhijie Chen","Yunjia Xu","Junfeng Jiang","Zefan Yu","Fei Cheng","Akiko Aizawa"],"published":"2026-11-12T11:03:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T11:03:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16630v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16630v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16630v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16608v1","title":"Towards Democratising Low-Data Medical Image Classification: CPU-Trainable ZACH-ViT and Its Geometric Variants","abstract":"Medical image classification in resource-constrained settings must contend with limited annotations and computational capacity. We evaluate four ZACH-ViT variants with linear, Euclidean prototype, Poincare and spherical heads alongside two compact multiple-instance-learning baselines. All models are trained from scratch with fewer than 260,000 parameters across seven MedMNIST+ datasets and three external benchmarks (PCam, ISIC2017 and Shenzhen tuberculosis radiographs). The low-data protocol uses 50 training images per class and five seeds, with validation-selected checkpoints at 23 and 47 epochs; a full-data reference uses one seed. Compact-TransMIL achieves the highest ten-dataset mean test Macro-F1 at 23 epochs (53.34%) and with full data (73.93%). At 47 epochs, ZACH-Linear narrowly leads (57.76% versus 57.54% for Compact-TransMIL). Extending training improves Macro-F1 in 239 of 300 paired comparisons. On ISIC2017, hZACH-Spherical has the highest 47-epoch low-data Macro-F1 (44.09% with SD 4.09%), whereas ZACH-EuclideanProto leads with full data (51.27%). We additionally conduct 40 CPU-only training runs on ISIC2017 and Shenzhen using four CPU threads; model-only single-image inference requires approximately 4-9 ms, while image decoding and preprocessing substantially increase end-to-end latency. The results demonstrate the feasibility of CPU-only training on the tested platform and the task- and regime-dependence of compact medical imaging under strict resource constraints.","authors":["Athanasios Angelakis"],"published":"2026-11-12T10:46:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T10:46:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16579v1","title":"ActWorld-JEPA: Action-Conditioned JEPA World Models Meet Latent Planning for End-to-End Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) enable future prediction in latent space without pixel reconstruction. However, latent predictive consistency alone does not establish the relative driving quality of candidate plans. We present ActWorld-JEPA, a joint-embedding formulation for candidate-based driving. Where Plan-Conditioned JEPA (PC-JEPA) uses global and step-wise trajectory intentions to guide recurrent latent prediction, then combines the predicted future with the current scene through gated cross-attention; Relational Residual Reranking ($R^3$) models agreement and discrepancy between plan and future representations under planning supervision, yielding bounded, gated residual corrections to the base planning scores. This formulation connects latent prediction to candidate selection using only observed context and proposed trajectories at inference. Benchmark-specific implementations report 94.1 PDMS on NAVSIM v1 navtest, with the final selector calibrated using aggregate NAVTEST feedback, and 91.6 and 42.7 EPDMS on NAVSIM v2 navtest and navhard, respectively. The nuScenes evaluation reports a mean $L_2$ error of 1.20 m and a predicted collision rate of 0.76% over 1--6 s. This work provides an effective path from JEPA's predictive representations to driving decisions informed by anticipated consequences.","authors":["Ziying Song","Shengkai Zhang","Caiyan Jia","Lei Yang","Lin Liu","Yuchen Liu","Ziyang Liu","Zirui Li","Gang Wang","Peiliang Wu","Li Wang","Yadan Luo","Chen Lv"],"published":"2026-11-12T10:10:39Z","updated":"2026-11-12T10:10:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16579v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16579v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16579v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16534v1","title":"Context-Predictive Low-Rank Adaptation for Face Presentation Attack Detection","abstract":"Face presentation attack detection requires representations that distinguish physical spoofing cues across changes in acquisition devices, subjects, and attack media. Large visual encoders offer transferable representations, but conventional binary fine-tuning supervises their final decisions without explicitly constraining how local facial evidence is represented under partial observation. We present CP-JEPA, a context-predictive adaptation framework that combines low-rank tuning of a pretrained CLIP image encoder with a joint-embedding predictive objective. A student predicts complete-image teacher features at masked spatial locations, while unmasked classification, masked classification, and prediction consistency retain the connection to the live/spoof task. An exponential-moving-average teacher supplies evolving latent targets. The auxiliary components are used only during training; inference requires only the LoRA-adapted CLIP visual encoder and binary classifier. {We evaluate CP-JEPA under single-source and multi-source OCIM transfer, additional CelebA-Spoof training, transfer to CelebA-Spoof, two-source training, and unseen-attack evaluation on SiW-Mv2.} On the standard OCIM benchmark, CP-JEPA achieves 3.74\\% mean HTER and 98.73\\% mean AUC. {Single-source evaluation achieves 11.36\\% mean HTER across twelve source--target combinations.} With CelebA-Spoof added to the three OCIM training sources, CP-JEPA reduces mean HTER from 3.45\\% to 2.35\\% and increases mean AUC from 98.68\\% to 99.32\\%. On SiW-Mv2, it achieves 3.36\\% mean HTER across the held-out attack categories. Controlled loss ablations examine the contributions of latent prediction, masked classification, and prediction consistency. The results show that context-predictive adaptation can improve cross-dataset PAD performance, although the gains vary with the training sources and evaluation setting.","authors":["Aminu Yusuf","Shakeel Muhammad Ibrahim","Sulaimon Oyeniyi Adebayo","Muhammad Sohail Ibrahim","Muhammad Shahid Jabbar","Shujaat Khan"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:32:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:32:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16534v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16534v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16534v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16517v1","title":"UniSpark: Exploring Hybrid Attention for Multimodal Understanding and Generation","abstract":"We present UniSpark-9B, a unified multimodal model that investigates hybrid attention for visual understanding and generation. Built on a Mixture-of-Transformer-Experts backbone with approximately 9B parameters in each branch, UniSpark combines full attention for explicit token interactions with GatedDeltaNet linear attention for compact context propagation. Autoregressive text prediction and visual flow matching jointly support visual question answering, text-to-image generation, instruction-guided editing, and interleaved text-image prediction. In interleaved generation, noisy visual targets are isolated from subsequent predictions, while clean visual representations provide context for later outputs. A blockwise protocol enforces this distinction through attention masks in full-attention layers and recurrent-state retention rules in linear-attention layers. The selected training sources contain 159.7M generation samples, 213.4M understanding samples, and 624.8K interleaved sequences. A four-stage curriculum jointly trains multimodal understanding and visual generation, refining answer accuracy and instruction following while extending image synthesis to 2K. UniSpark scores 2470.3 on MME-S and 0.83 on GenEval. Qualitative examples further illustrate its capabilities in interleaved image generation, video synthesis, and video editing. Together, these results demonstrate that hybrid attention provides an effective backbone for unified multimodal understanding and generation.","authors":["Jiancheng Pan","Yuzheng Liu","Hengyuan Cao","Qiyuan Zhang","Yangyan Li","Biao Gong","Zheng Zhang","Ka Long Cheng","Xinyang Wang","Yujun Shen"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:16:10Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:16:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16517v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16517v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16517v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16515v1","title":"PiLLVD: A Real-World Low-Light Video Dataset for Dynamic Scenes Captured with Off-the-Shelf Cameras","abstract":"Low-light image and video enhancement has received increasing attention in recent years. However, most existing enhancement methods are developed and evaluated on still images, while progress in low-light video enhancement remains limited by the lack of real-world paired video datasets. Acquiring paired low-light and normal-light video sequences is challenging because it requires spatially and temporally aligned captures while avoiding motion blur, exposure mismatch, and correspondence errors in dynamic scenes. To address this gap, we introduce PiLLVD, the Pi-based Low-Light Video Dataset, a paired low-light video dataset captured using an accessible Raspberry Pi-based multi-camera system with off-the-shelf cameras. PiLLVD provides paired low-light and aligned normal-light reference sequences for dynamic indoor and outdoor scenes, including diverse human and object motion under varying illumination conditions. Unlike existing datasets that are often image-based, static, synthetic, or limited to simple motion patterns, PiLLVD focuses on motion-rich scenes where frame-to-frame stability and motion-related artefacts are important for evaluation. In addition to presenting the dataset and acquisition pipeline, we benchmark representative pre-trained low-light video enhancement methods on PiLLVD under a zero-shot setting. The results show that existing methods do not generalise uniformly across the proposed sequences, highlighting the challenges posed by the proposed dataset and its value for both benchmarking existing methods and supporting the development of future low-light video enhancement algorithms.","authors":["Yaser Abu Awwad","Paul L. Rosin","Oktay Karakuş"],"published":"2026-11-12T09:13:12Z","updated":"2026-11-12T09:13:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16515v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16515v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16515v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16452v1","title":"Can an LVLM Perceive Event Boundaries Like a Human?","abstract":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) reason effectively about video content, but whether they perceive its event boundaries in a manner consistent with humans remains unclear. We first investigate this through Generic Event Boundary Detection (GEBD), which identifies event boundaries naturally perceived by humans in continuous video, without relying on predefined event categories. On Kinetics-GEBD dataset, LVLMs miss many boundaries when predicting directly from frames, but effectively refine candidates proposed by an unsupervised discovery method, achieving state-of-the-art unsupervised GEBD performance. We extend this discovery-refinement approach to hour-long egocentric video, where boundary discovery requires a broader semantic scale and whole-video LVLM refinement becomes impractical. We address these challenges through coarse-to-fine temporal discovery and localized LVLM accept/reject judgments. Human evaluation shows that the LVLM's agreement with group consensus falls within the range observed among human annotators, with disagreements concentrated on low-consensus cases. Using this pipeline, we construct EgoBoundary, a benchmark for event boundary detection in hour-scale egocentric video. Representative supervised and unsupervised methods degrade substantially on EgoBoundary, highlighting the challenge of long-horizon event boundary detection.","authors":["Hyolim Kang","Jungsoo Lee","Yeji Song","Whie Jung","Janghoon Cho","Juntae Lee"],"published":"2026-11-12T08:06:48Z","updated":"2026-11-12T08:06:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16452v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16452v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16452v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16451v1","title":"HIFC-IQA: Train-Free Cross-Domain Image Quality Assessment via Dual-Process Cognition","abstract":"The gap between controlled synthetic distortions and complex real-world degradations introduces a critical domain shift challenge for No-Reference Image Quality Assessment. Although Unsupervised Domain Adaptation (UDA) approaches attempt to address this issue, they remain constrained by substantial retraining overhead and cumulative error propagation inherent in iterative pseudo-labeling. Inspired by the dual-process theory of human cognition for remarkable generalization ability, we propose Hierarchical Intuitive and Fuzzy Consensus (HIFC), a completely train-free Cross-Domain IQA framework. HIFC employs asymmetric perceptual decomposition to disentangle semantic content from distortion-sensitive representations. The final quality score is fused through the synergy of two complementary branches: a memory consensus branch that retrieves a stable Fuzzy Quality Centroid from a synthetic reference gallery, and an intuitive branch that captures aesthetic polarization via zero-shot vision-language alignment. Extensive experiments show that HIFC achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple benchmarks, attaining the highest average SRCC and PLCC among competing UDA methods. Most notably, in the challenging synthetic-to-authentic cross-domain setting, HIFC achieves a substantial performance margin over competing methods, demonstrating remarkable robustness and generalization.","authors":["Yu Li","Zhengran Shen","Puchao Zhou","Yukang Ding","Yachun Mi","Boyuan Yang","Xi Zhai","Shaohui Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T08:04:40Z","updated":"2026-11-12T08:04:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16451v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16451v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16451v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16424v1","title":"PhysCapBench: A Cross-Domain Benchmark for Physically Grounded Video Captioning","abstract":"Physical AI aims to learn transferable physical knowledge from real-world experience to understand, predict, and act in the physical world. Internet videos and embodied demonstrations provide complementary observations of motion, interaction, and state evolution. A foundational data challenge is to measure and preserve this physical information as training supervision. In video-language pretraining, captions express observed processes in language, yet a semantically correct description can omit the interactions and transitions that define those processes. Rich physical experience in video does not automatically translate into sufficient language supervision. This gap calls for a yardstick to assess annotation quality, compare captioning strategies, and guide training-data refinement. Motivated by our data construction for world-model pretraining, we introduce PhysCapBench, to our knowledge the first unified physical-caption benchmark spanning all three domains: third-person scenes, egocentric manipulation, and handheld-gripper demonstrations. It provides human-verified annotations for 1,500 video sequences and evaluates accuracy and coverage through seven physical dimensions. We examine downstream utility through video reconstruction and manipulation-policy fine-tuning. Physical captions reduce reconstruction LPIPS by 8.6-9.5% on third-person videos and 26.9-30.9% on egocentric videos relative to metadata captions. Training-instruction enrichment improves both PhysCapBench scores and policy success, including a 7.5-percentage-point gain for pi0.5 on a RoboTwin 2.0 subtask. PhysCapBench makes the physical content of language supervision inspectable and comparable, providing a cross-domain basis for its evaluation and improvement.","authors":["Haofeng Wang","Pengcheng Wen","Ruiqi Li","Yuhao Zhang","Tiange Zhang","Boren Zheng","Xuanyi Liu","Siyu Tao","Danni Yu","Gan Lin","Haocheng Tang","Tianzizhe Guo","Mingyuan Su","Boyuan Chen","Zhongyuan Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:33:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:33:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16424v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16424v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16424v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16418v1","title":"Model Distribution-Aware Multimodal Dataset Distillation","abstract":"Multimodal dataset distillation (MDD) aims to reduce the cost of vision-language pre-training by synthesizing compact image-text datasets. Although recent feature-matching methods have improved distillation efficiency, synthetic data may become specialized to the model representations encountered during optimization, limiting generalization to unseen model states. We propose Model Distribution-Aware Multimodal Dataset Distillation (MDA-DD), which leverages supervision from a dynamic staggered queue of models at different training stages. We further introduce asymmetric dual projection for complementary alignment in visual and linguistic spaces and an uncentered second-order moment objective that retains mean-related cross-modal information. Experiments on Flickr30K and MS-COCO demonstrate consistent retrieval improvements over existing methods across synthetic-data budgets. With only 100 synthetic pairs, MDA-DD improves IR@1 and TR@1 on Flickr30K by 12.9 and 18.2 percentage points, respectively, over the strongest baseline at the same budget, outperforming all compared methods using 500 pairs on both datasets across all six retrieval metrics.","authors":["Jiajun Shen","Yonglin Wu","Ruonan Yu","Xinchao Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:29:09Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:29:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16418v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16418v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16418v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16417v1","title":"VBot Exo2Ego Policy: Lightweight Data Collection and Exocentric-to-Egocentric Video Generation","abstract":"We present VBot Exo2Ego Policy, a video generation model that turns third-person videos into first-person observations of a selected actor, offering a practical way to expand first-person operation data from abundant real-world footage. To support this goal, we introduce a lightweight data collection pipeline and mobile app that support first-person recording, third-person recording, and synchronized capture of both views. This setup combines accessible single-view collection with paired RGB and camera geometry for learning cross-view correspondence. Building on a pretrained video diffusion transformer, our model incorporates geometric guidance and auxiliary supervision from semantic masks and hand keypoints to facilitate optimization and improve the fidelity of hand-object interactions. Camera geometry is injected stochastically during training and gradually removed, allowing inference without target-camera calibration. To accommodate the diversity of Internet videos, we combine source-video perception and reasoning, optional actor references, and multi-shot adaptation to identify the intended actor and follow an operation across camera cuts. The collection system and generation model provide a foundation for expanding first-person operation data across tasks, scenes, and participants.","authors":["Tong Rao","Zhihao Zhang","Yuntian Wang","Siyu Zhou","Hailong Qin","Yinan Yu"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:27:47Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:27:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16417v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16417v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16417v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16413v1","title":"RICapBench: A Fine-Grained Visual Understanding Benchmark Dataset for Vision-Language Models in Remote Sensing","abstract":"Advances in vision-language models (VLMs) have improved remote sensing image analysis and supported geospatial decision-making. However, fine-grained understanding of remote sensing imagery remains challenging for VLMs. Large variations in object scale, complex geospatial relations among instances, and limited visual detail hinder precise instance localization, visible attribute identification, and spatial relation understanding, potentially leading to descriptions that lack visual grounding. Existing datasets and benchmarks insufficiently assess end-to-end instance localization and captioning without target-specific textual cues. To support this evaluation, we introduce RICapBench, a dataset and benchmark that unifies instance localization and fine-grained captioning in remote sensing. RICapBench contains 245,466 instance-level annotations and 498,005 spatial relation tuples spanning general remote sensing, UAV remote sensing, and dense small-object scenarios. Each annotation pairs an object's oriented bounding box with a natural language description of its visible attributes and spatial relations to surrounding instances and environmental elements. To evaluate the accuracy and completeness of spatial relation captioning, we propose a remote sensing spatial relation parser and the RelCap-F1 metric. We combine RelCap-F1 with instance localization and attribute captioning metrics into a composite metric, RICScore. Our evaluation of 15 advanced open-source and closed-source VLMs identifies instance localization as a major bottleneck for these models. LocateAnything achieves the highest RICScore of 31.68, but scores lower on spatial relation captioning than other task-fine-tuned open-source VLMs. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/Xldln/RICapBench.","authors":["Xue Wang","Yubin Xu","Kun Tan"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:24:49Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:24:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16413v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16413v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16413v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16402v1","title":"Teach with Blinks: Breaking Linguistic Momentum in On-Policy Distillation for Fine-Grained Multimodal Perception","abstract":"On-policy distillation (OPD) has emerged as an effective post-training approach to improving fine-grained perception in multimodal large language models by amortizing costly inference-time zooming operations into a single-pass recipe. However, vanilla OPD is inherently susceptible to linguistic momentum: the teacher can follow the student's erroneous prefixes, thus weakening corrective supervision. To address this, we propose Blink On-Policy Distillation (BlinkOPD), in which the teacher \"blinks\" at every token: it reads the same prefix once with its eyes open and once with its eyes closed. A first-glance gain further strengthens the correction on rollouts that conflict with the teacher. By extracting more effective supervision from each on-policy trajectory, BlinkOPD achieves state-of-the-art average performance across six fine-grained benchmarks using only 500 training images (12x less data) than existing OPD methods. Beyond fine-grained perception, BlinkOPD also demonstrates strong zero-shot improvements across ten held-out benchmarks spanning general visual perception, visual hallucination, and multimodal reasoning abilities. Code: https://github.com/Supltz/BlinkOPD","authors":["Tangzheng Lian","Hang Zhang","Guanyu Hu","Renjiu Hu","Zhensong Zhang","Jiankang Deng","Jifei Song"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:11:20Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:11:20Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16402v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16402v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16402v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16400v1","title":"FuGIBench: Evaluating Reliable Preparation in Streaming Video","abstract":"An assistant watching a worker can prepare for an emerging need before the worker is ready to receive help.As the need evolves, the assistant must maintain preparation until a response is due, or withdraw it if the need disappears. Video anticipation and interactive response benchmarks leave this interval between early evidence and response largely unmeasured. We introduce FuGIBench (Future-Grounded Interaction), a streaming-video benchmark in which a model chooses among waiting, reversible preparation, and response given a causal video prefix and a task contract. The benchmark contains 19,893 synthetic tool-assistance videos across ten tool tasks, in handover, ordinary-activity, and cancelled-need tracks. One primary score credits sustained preparation, incorporates response-interval alignment, and penalizes activity when the reference prescribes waiting. On a frozen set of 1,000 episodes, a reference system with a longer preparation lead has poorer response alignment, while similar handover leads accompany different levels of activity during ordinary work in matched scenarios. Together with cancellation analyses, these results show why evaluating preparation requires tracking decisions as needs develop, become actionable, or disappear.","authors":["Changheng Cai","Zhichao Huang","Song Ren","Zhichen Zhao","Hongsen Liu","Chaoyang Zheng","Wenxi Wu","Yuan Fang","Wei Wang","Yang Wang","Yuchen Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T07:10:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T07:10:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16400v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16400v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16400v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16373v1","title":"Description-Guided CLIP Adaptation with Local Evidence Calibration for Few-Shot Plant Disease Recognition","abstract":"Accurate plant disease recognition is essential for intelligent agriculture, but few-shot settings provide limited labeled samples that may not capture diagnostic symptoms and intra-class variations. Pretrained vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, offer transferable visual-semantic knowledge. However, existing CLIP-based methods often rely on coarse class semantics and global visual representations, overlooking disease-specific diagnostic information and fine-grained local cues. Excessive few-shot adaptation may also cause over-specialization and weaken the generalization ability of large-scale pre-training. We propose a description-guided, two-stage CLIP adaptation method to address these issues. Symptom and comparative descriptions enrich class semantics with typical visual symptoms and discriminative cues between similar diseases. The enhanced semantics guide the aggregation of class-relevant patch evidence to calibrate global image-text matching with fine-grained cues. For adaptation, we first update the vision-language encoders with lightweight parameters, then freeze them and train a linear classifier. At inference, the classifier is used for in-domain prediction, while class prototypes from the adapted text encoder support cross-domain prediction and retain vision-language transferability. Experiments with two backbones show that our method achieves the highest mean accuracy in most few-shot settings on PlantVillage, Cassava, and Pear, reaching 93.16%, 79.30%, and 96.56%, respectively, with ViT-L/14 under 16-shot. On class-aligned cross-domain evaluation across 21 shared PlantDoc classes, it achieves 56.96% accuracy, outperforming all compared methods. These results demonstrate its effectiveness in fine-grained few-shot plant disease recognition and cross-domain generalization.","authors":["Zhenkun Ye","Qian Zhou","Hua Zou"],"published":"2026-11-12T06:40:54Z","updated":"2026-11-12T06:40:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16373v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16373v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16373v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16339v1","title":"Hand in Silhouette: Synthetic-to-Real Multi-View Hand Reconstruction via Binary Masks","abstract":"Calibrated multi-view hand reconstruction has advanced rapidly, yet the effectiveness of existing methods depends strongly on real captured data, which is costly to acquire, limited in hand-geometry diversity, and often affected by imperfect annotations. Synthetic data offers a scalable alternative with accurate supervision, but a substantial synthetic-to-real appearance gap hinders its use. To address this, we introduce Hand in Silhouette (SilHand), a calibrated multi-view hand reconstruction framework that operates on binary hand masks, obtainable at low cost either automatically or manually, to reduce the synthetic-to-real observation gap. Rather than relying on RGB features for 3D lifting, SilHand projects metric 3D hypotheses into each view to query and fuse mask-ray evidence for hand reconstruction. This mask-centric formulation enables training on a large-scale synthetic dataset comprising 46.8k grasping poses and 60k free-hand samples, providing broad coverage of hand geometry while directly transferring to real captured hand geometry. Across five real-world benchmarks, SilHand improves relative reconstruction accuracy from 49.2% to 63.4% using masks rendered from reference hands. Moreover, reconstruction accuracy improves consistently as the synthetic training set grows, indicating that broader geometric coverage translates into better real-world performance. While achieving such diversity is difficult with real captured data, it becomes practical under our mask-centric formulation. Finally, SilHand remains robust under mask corruption, with reconstruction error increasing by at most 1.63 mm. Together, these results support binary masks as an effective interface for learning hand reconstruction from synthetic geometry.","authors":["Leiyao Cui","Lecheng Ruan","Zihang Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:59:25Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:59:25Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16339v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16339v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16339v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16310v1","title":"Selective Generative Restoration for Video Deraining via Uncertainty-Aware Diffusion","abstract":"Video deraining is challenging due to rain streaks, occlusions, and atmospheric effects. Existing methods mainly follow a deterministic restoration paradigm, assuming that clean scenes can be uniquely recovered from rainy observations. However, rain degradation is spatially heterogeneous. Reliable regions require faithful reconstruction, whereas severely occluded regions contain ambiguous content that require stronger prior-driven inference. Therefore, conventional deterministic restoration often produces over-smoothed results, while global generative restoration may introduce unnecessary hallucinations. We introduce a Selective Generative Restoration paradigm for video deraining, where generative priors are adaptively activated according to restoration uncertainty. Based on this principle, we propose Selective Generative Video Deraining (SGVD), an uncertainty-aware diffusion framework that enables adaptive fidelity preservation and generative recovery. Specifically, SGVD exploits stochastic diversity between dual one-step diffusion predictions to estimate region-level restoration uncertainty, which determines where observation fidelity should be preserved and where generative inference should be activated. Furthermore, an anchor-guided trajectory consistency mechanism is proposed to regularize stochastic diffusion evolution across frames, reducing temporal generation drift and improving video coherence. To achieve practical efficiency, SGVD adopts one-step diffusion inference while maintaining uncertainty-aware generative capability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SGVD achieves superior restoration performance, perceptual quality, and temporal consistency compared with state-of-the-art video deraining methods.","authors":["Yi'ang Chen","Han Li","Laibin Chang","Hui Liu","Kui Jiang","Junjun Jiang"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:10:53Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:10:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16310v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16310v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16310v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16307v1","title":"DEDCA: Test-Time Adaptation for Generalized AI-Generated Image Detection","abstract":"AI-generated image detectors face significant challenges when deployed in real-world environments, particularly when test samples are produced by unseen generators that deviate from the source training distribution. Existing detectors often rely on a single type of evidence, such as semantic representations or low-level generation traces, making them vulnerable when the corresponding cue becomes unreliable. Moreover, adapting detectors to newly emerging generators usually requires target labels or retraining, which is costly and impractical. To address these challenges, we propose Dual-Evidence Disagreement-Constrained Adaptation (DEDCA), a test-time adaptation framework for generalized AI-generated image detection. DEDCA constructs a dual-evidence detector by combining CLIP-based semantic evidence with trace evidence extracted from shuffled entropy and residual statistics, and introduces a reliability-aware gate to perform sample-specific adaptive fusion. Our key idea is to exploit the disagreement between the two evidence streams as an unlabeled adaptation signal, enabling the detector to adjust to target-domain shifts without relying on target labels or blindly trusting a single prediction stream. Experiments show that DEDCA achieves superior performance on the GenImage benchmark by outperforming state-of-the-art AI-generated image detectors and test-time adaptation methods with 92.0\\% average accuracy.","authors":["Hao Tan","Qimin Zhang","Lihuang Fang","Kebing Jin","Jinghui Qin"],"published":"2026-11-12T05:07:23Z","updated":"2026-11-12T05:07:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16307v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16307v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16307v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16269v1","title":"FlowIR: Depth-driven Rectified Flow for Resolving Thermal Ambiguity in Infrared Super-Resolution","abstract":"Distinguishing different heat sources in low-resolution infrared (IR) images is inherently difficult, as multiple thermal emitters often collapse into ambiguous and overlapping responses. Existing super-resolution methods hallucinate structures from such ambiguous 2D evidence, leading to thermal drift, boundary merging, and unreliable restoration under infrared degradations. We propose FlowIR, a depth-driven rectified flow framework that resolves thermal ambiguity by injecting 3D geometric cues into the generation process. Pseudo-3D depth lifting and depth-guided 3D convolutions disentangle overlapping foreground objects, while cluster-wise inter-attention suppresses cross-source feature contamination. A Thermal-Geometry Consistency Loss further enforces geometric alignment during refinement. Built upon a lightweight rectified flow backbone, FlowIR restores IR images in only a few steps, producing sharply separated, degradation-robust, and geometrically faithful high-resolution results.","authors":["Xingyue Zhu","Qinyun Mei","Zhexi Min","Yang Zou","Xingyuan Li","Zhiying Jiang","Jinyuan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-12T04:15:32Z","updated":"2026-11-12T04:15:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16269v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16269v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16269v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16259v1","title":"Reconstruct What You Can: Distractor-free 4D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"Dynamic reconstruction from casual video must preserve valid subject motion while suppressing transient occluders, shadows, and volumetric effects. Existing methods either suppress valid motion through static-scene assumptions or model all observations jointly, producing ghosting and unstable geometry. We introduce ReCan-GS, a 4D Gaussian Splatting framework that separates coherent scene content from inconsistent observations. It derives a Jacobian-based deformation-sensitivity score from the learned deformation field, using it as an empirical proxy for reconstruction ambiguity. The score guides separation into dynamic Core Gaussians and per-view Distractor Gaussians, or post-hoc pruning of sensitive Core Gaussians. The same criterion supports removal of spatial occluders, photometric artifacts, and evolving smoke or fog without masks, pretrained segmenters, or medium-specific supervision. Experiments on synthetic and real dynamic scenes and synthetic smoke/fog scenes demonstrate its usefulness across the evaluated distractor regimes.","authors":["Joel Julin","Liuyue Xie","Ananya Bal","Mose Sakashita","Koichiro Niinuma","Laszlo A. Jeni"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:59:45Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:59:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16259v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16259v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16259v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16224v1","title":"Disentangled Embedding Optimization for Diffusion-Based Dataset Distillation","abstract":"Dataset distillation aims to compress large-scale datasets into compact synthetic subsets that enable efficient model training with comparable performance to the original data. Recent advances show that diffusion models are highly effective for this task due to their strong generative capability. Existing diffusion-based approaches typically perform class-wise clustering and use the obtained cluster prototypes as diffusion inputs to generate distilled samples. However, the uniformly distributed original samples in a category inevitably lead to entanglement near cluster boundaries, pulling the cluster prototypes closer and reducing the inter-cluster separation. Consequently, the diffusion model receives overly similar latent inputs, resulting in synthetic data with limited diversity. To address this issue, we propose Disentangled Embedding Optimization (DEO), a simple yet effective framework that replaces static clustering with trainable embeddings. These embeddings are optimized to match the Gaussian statistics (i.e., mean and covariance) of the original data distribution, while a diversity regularizer is designed to encourage separation among these embeddings to improve the intra-class variability. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our proposed DEO can generate more diversified and representative distilled data than existing cluster-based diffusion methods, achieving state-of-the-art performance and superior generalization across multiple datasets.","authors":["Hongcheng Li","Yucan Zhou","Yibin Wang","Xiaoyan Gu","Bo Li","Weiping Wang"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:18:19Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:18:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16224v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16224v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16224v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16217v1","title":"WorldDraft: If Code Builds the World, Can Video Models Just Render It?","abstract":"Multimodal coding agents decouple 4D world construction from visual generation, repurposing video models from world generators toward generative renderers. This division of labor raises a fundamental question: if code builds the world, can video models just render it? A reliable generative renderer hinges on two complementary criteria: (i) world preservation, retaining prescribed 4D kinematics and camera paths; and (ii) visual realization, persistently binding text-specified target attributes over time. To evaluate this capability, we introduce WorldDraft, a benchmark of $432$ strictly paired test cases across $48$ executable 4D world families spanning six dynamic challenge categories. Under a $3 \\times 3$ factorial design, WorldDraft orthogonally crosses draft granularity (abstract proxy to previs) and appearance alignment (neutral to conflicting) under identical dynamics. Extensive evaluations across $12$ representative models reveal that: (I) world preservation and visual realization remain in stark operational tension; (II) richer draft scaffolding anchors structure but paradoxically impairs appearance fidelity; and (III) conflicting source cues trigger severe source leakage (up to $64\\%$), exposing fragile cross-modal disentanglement. To mitigate these bottlenecks, we propose WorldDraft-Primer, a plug-and-play visual prompting strategy that consistently improves both objectives across frozen video renderers. Together, WorldDraft and WorldDraft-Primer lay a foundation for bridging programmatic 4D simulation with video generation, advancing physically grounded generative rendering.","authors":["Zhiyu Hou","Wen-Jie Shu","Harold Haodong Chen","Weilin Ruan","Chenfei Liao","Liuzhou Zhang","Disen Lan","Xiaogang Xu","Qi Chen","Ser-Nam Lim"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:09:18Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:09:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16217v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16217v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16217v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16212v1","title":"SPEM: Scan-Prior Entropy Modeling for LiDAR Point Cloud Compression","abstract":"LiDAR point clouds exhibit scan-induced spatial regularities that are informative for geometry compression. Existing scan-aware approaches often exploit these regularities by transforming coded geometry into spherical or beam-aligned coordinate systems. However, this couples acquisition priors with geometry representation, altering spatial partitioning and quantization, and potentially preventing exact recovery of the original Cartesian geometry. We argue that acquisition priors should guide probability modeling rather than dictate the coding representation. We propose SPEM, a Scan-Prior Entropy Modeling framework that exploits LiDAR scanning regularities within the entropy model without requiring coordinate transformation. We introduce a multi-frequency spherical rotary positional encoding that captures scan-aligned spatial relationships in both backbone self-attention and decoded-context retrieval. Building upon this encoding, we develop a decoded-memory autoregressive predictor that efficiently exploits previously decoded occupancy information through reusable key/value caches. Experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art performance in both lossy and lossless LiDAR geometry compression, highlighting the effectiveness of scan-prior modeling across different coordinate representations.","authors":["Bojun Liu","Yangzhi Ma","Jie Li","Dong Liu","Li Li"],"published":"2026-11-12T03:05:57Z","updated":"2026-11-12T03:05:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"},{"id":"2611.16162v1","title":"GOOSELoc: A Multimodal Benchmark for Ground-to-Aerial Localization in Unstructured Off-Road Terrain","abstract":"Cross-view pose estimation estimates a ground camera's geographic pose by matching onboard sensor data with geo-referenced overhead imagery. This is particularly relevant when GNSS is unreliable, for example under forest canopy or in urban canyons. Existing metric cross-view benchmarks, such as VIGOR and cross-view KITTI, are dominated by urban and road scenes with strong human-made structure without off-road terrain. We introduce GOOSELoc, a multimodal benchmark for ground-to-aerial localization in unstructured environments, including forests, fields, and rural tracks. GOOSELoc comprises 9,356 scenes recorded across all four seasons around Munich, Germany, pairing geo-referenced aerial orthophotos with surround-view RGB imagery and 3D LiDAR point clouds. Paved/unpaved labels enable analyzing the impact of terrain structure, while the seasonal recordings allow studying the appearance gap to mostly summer-captured orthophotos. To assess the transferability of existing methods, we adapt and fine-tune four state-of-the-art cross-view localization baselines on GOOSELoc. Despite task-specific fine-tuning, the performance degrades substantially in off-road environments. We therefore propose a multimodal baseline combining surround-view imagery and LiDAR geometry, improving localization performance and providing a stronger reference for future work. Data and baselines are released at https://gooseloc.github.io.","authors":["Fabian Deuser","Peter Mortimer","Robin-Nico Kampa","Anna Bößendörfer","Mirko Mählisch","Martin Werner","Norbert Oswald"],"published":"2026-11-12T01:49:38Z","updated":"2026-11-12T01:49:38Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16162v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.16162v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16162v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-13T07:32:40+00:00"}],"tracked_paper_count":57,"storage_mode":"latest_overwrite"}};
